export const ARTICLES = {
  zetaoph: { text: "Zeta Ophiuchi is a hot blue star in the constellation Ophiuchus. It lies roughly 400 to 440 light years from Earth. No O-type star, the class of very hot blue stars, lies closer to our Solar System. From Earth it shines at magnitude 2.6. Only two stars in its constellation look brighter. In April 2010 the asteroid 824 Anastasia passed in front of it and hid it for a moment.\n\nIt is heavy, at about 19 to 20 M☉, and about eight times as wide as the Sun. It gives out roughly 49,000 times as much light as the Sun. Its surface is near 34,300 K. It spins very fast, perhaps once a day, which is close to the speed that would pull it apart; its equator may race along at up to 400 km each second. The fast spin leaves the poles hotter than the middle: about 39,000 K at the poles and 30,700 K at the equator.\n\nDust in space between here and the star soaks up much of its light, most of all the blue light. Without that dust, Zeta Ophiuchi would reach magnitude 1.54 and rank with the brightest stars we can see.\n\nZeta Ophiuchi is a runaway star. It rushes through space at about 30 km each second. It came from Upper Scorpius, a group of young stars that were born together and still drift the same way. It probably once had a heavier partner. When that partner blew up in a supernova, a huge star explosion, Zeta Ophiuchi was flung away. A pulsar, PSR B1929+10, could be what is left of that partner. As the star pushes through thick dust, it piles up a curved wall of gas ahead of it, called a bow shock. NASA’s WISE telescope pictured it in infrared light. The star feeds this wall with its own wind: it loses about a ten-millionth of the Sun’s mass each year, as much as the whole Sun every nine million years.\n\nIn 1979, astronomers saw odd moving bumps in the star’s light when they split it into colours. Stars with the same pattern are now called Zeta Oph stars. The bumps are probably waves that ripple unevenly across the surface. Its brightness also rises and falls with a dozen or more beats, between one and ten times a day. The star is young, aged six million years or a bit more, and about halfway through the first stage of its life. In a few million years it will grow into a red supergiant so big it would reach past Jupiter’s orbit. Then it will explode and leave a neutron star behind.", photos: [{"src": "photos/zetaoph.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Sig12-014.png/960px-Sig12-014.png", "alt": "Infrared image from the Spitzer Space Telescope of Zeta Ophiuchi, a bright star with a curved bow shock of glowing dust in front of it.", "credit": "NASA/JPL-Caltech / Spitzer, public domain", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/zetaoph-toy.webp", "alt": "Zeta Ophiuchi as this toy draws it", "credit": "Zeta Ophiuchi in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/zetaoph-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Zeta_Ophiuchi_Star_from_the_Observatorio_El_Sauce_Harris_Telescope_courtesy_Adam_Block.jpg/960px-Zeta_Ophiuchi_Star_from_the_Observatorio_El_Sauce_Harris_Telescope_courtesy_Adam_Block.jpg", "alt": "Zeta Ophiuchi as a bright white star in red clouds of dust and gas, with a curved blue arc of gas sweeping round it, photographed through a 24 inch telescope in Chile.", "credit": "Adam Block, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 2}] },
  regulus: { text: "Regulus shines brighter than any other star in Leo, the Lion, and it ranks among the brightest points of light in our sky, at magnitude 1.35. It lies about 79 light years away. Its name is Latin for \"little king\" or \"prince\". With five dimmer stars it makes the Sickle, a hook shape that marks the lion’s head. Regulus sits less than half a degree from the ecliptic, the line the Sun, Moon and planets follow across the sky, so the Moon often slides in front of it.\n\nTo the eye it looks like one star, but there are four, in two pairs. The main star, Regulus A, is blue-white and about 4.15 times as massive as the Sun. It spins so fast that one turn takes only 15.9 hours, while the Sun needs about 25 days. Its equator races along at about 320 km per second, 96.5 % of the speed that would tear it apart. The spin squashes it into a flattened shape, so the poles, at about 14,500 K, are hotter and brighter than the middle, at about 11,000 K. In 2017 a team led by Daniel Cotton found that the squashed star gives out polarised light, light whose waves shake mostly in one direction.\n\nA small, faint partner circles Regulus A in about 40 days. It weighs about 0.3 times as much as the Sun. In 2020 a team led by Douglas Gies picked out its faint light in the spectrum of Regulus: it is a young white dwarf, the cooling core of a dead star. Far out, about 5,000 AU away, a second pair, an orange dwarf and a red dwarf, go round each other in roughly 600 years. Farther still, a brown dwarf called SDSS J1007+1930 moves through space with Regulus. If it truly belongs to the system, one orbit would take about 200 million years.\n\nPeople have watched this star for thousands of years. To the Babylonians it was Sharru, \"the King\", and the Arabic name Qalb al-Asad means \"heart of the lion\". Astronomers once thought Regulus A was only 50 to 100 million years old. A white dwarf partner means the system must be a billion years old or more, perhaps because Regulus A once took gas from its partner and grew.\n\nPlanets rarely cover it. Venus last passed in front of Regulus on 7 July 1959 and will do so again on 1 October 2044. Asteroids sometimes do. When 166 Rhodope crossed it on 19 October 2005, observers in Italy filmed the event, and the tiny bending of the starlight matched Einstein’s general relativity.", photos: [{"src": "photos/regulus.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/Regulus_1.JPG", "alt": "Regulus as a bright point of light, photographed through an amateur telescope.", "credit": "Drew Farwell, CC BY-SA 3.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/regulus-toy.webp", "alt": "Regulus as this toy draws it", "credit": "Regulus in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/regulus-2.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a8/LeoCC.jpg", "alt": "A night-sky photo of the constellation Leo with its stick figure drawn in; Regulus sits at the lower right corner of the figure, with the planet Jupiter shining orange below it in March 2004.", "credit": "Till Credner, CC BY-SA 3.0", "after": 4}] },
  vega: { text: "Vega is the brightest star in Lyra, the Lyre, and the fifth brightest star in the night sky. It lies about 25 light years away, close by the measure of the galaxy. With Deneb and Altair it makes the Summer Triangle, a large shape that is easy to find on summer evenings in the north.\n\nIt is a young white star, about twice as heavy as the Sun and some 700 million years old. A heavier star burns its fuel faster, so Vega will live only about a tenth as long as the Sun. Its core is hotter than the Sun’s, so most of its energy comes from the CNO cycle, a chain of reactions in which carbon, nitrogen and oxygen help turn hydrogen into helium. It also holds fewer heavy elements than the Sun does, only about a third as much.\n\nVega spins fast, once in about 16 hours. At its equator the surface moves at about 236 km each second, 88 % of the speed that would break it apart. The spin pushes its middle outward, so the star is 19 % wider at its equator than from pole to pole. Its poles are the hottest part, about 10,000 K, and its equator is cooler, about 8,900 K. From the Earth we look almost straight down onto one pole, so to us it looks round and even. The CHARA array, a group of linked telescopes on Mount Wilson, confirmed this picture in 2005 and 2006.\n\nVega is a star of firsts. In July 1850 William Bond and John Adams Whipple made it the first star after the Sun to be photographed, on a daguerreotype, an early photo on a silvered plate, at Harvard College Observatory. In 1872 Henry Draper photographed its spectrum, the first picture of a star’s light to show dark lines. Friedrich von Struve published one of the first star distances for it: a tiny yearly shift in its position of 0.125 arcseconds, close to the 0.129 that the Hipparcos satellite measured much later. For a long time astronomers used Vega to set the zero of the brightness scale.\n\nThe Earth’s axis slowly wobbles, so the pole star changes over thousands of years. Around 12,000 BCE Vega stood close to the north celestial pole, and it will come near the pole again in about 12,000 years.\n\nIn 1983 the IRAS satellite found more infrared light from Vega than a star alone gives. The extra light comes from a wide disc of dust round it, made as rocks collide, like the Sun’s Kuiper belt. Stars with such discs are now called Vega-like. In 2024 the Webb and Hubble telescopes showed the disc to be very smooth, with no clear sign of large planets shaping it.", photos: [{"src": "photos/vega.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Hubble_and_Webb_Observations_of_Vega_Circumstellar_Disk_%282024-030%29.jpg/960px-Hubble_and_Webb_Observations_of_Vega_Circumstellar_Disk_%282024-030%29.jpg", "alt": "Hubble and Webb views of the wide, smooth dust disc round Vega, the star masked at the centre", "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI / Hubble and Webb, public domain", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/vega-toy.webp", "alt": "Vega as this toy draws it", "credit": "Vega in this toy", "after": 4}, {"src": "photos/vega-2.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8a/The_first_image_of_Vega.jpg", "alt": "The first photograph of a star: Vega as a small bright point on a dark, worn daguerreotype plate from Harvard College Observatory, 1850.", "credit": "John Adams Whipple, public domain", "after": 3}] },
  sirius: { text: "No other star at night looks as bright as Sirius. It shines in Canis Major, the Greater Dog, a southern constellation next to Orion. It is only about 8.6 light years away, one of our closest neighbours among the stars. Its name comes from an old Greek word that means glowing or scorching.\n\nSirius is really two stars. The bright one, Sirius A, is a white star with about twice the Sun’s mass and a surface near 9,940 K, and it gives out about 25 times as much light as the Sun. Its partner, Sirius B, is a white dwarf: the small, hot core that is left when a star dies. Sirius B is about as wide as the Earth, some 12,000 km, yet it holds about 1.02 times the Sun’s mass, as the Hubble Space Telescope showed in 2005. The two go around each other once every 50 years, on a stretched path that brings them from 8.2 to 31.5 AU apart.\n\nLong ago, Sirius B was the heavier of the pair. It burned through its fuel, swelled into a red giant, threw off its outer layers and shrank. That happened some 120 million years ago, and the system as a whole is 200 to 300 million years old. With no fire left inside, Sirius B now only cools. Its surface is still about 25,200 K, and in X-rays the Chandra X-ray Observatory sees it outshine Sirius A.\n\nIn Ancient Egypt, the first dawn rising of Sirius each year told people that the Nile flood was coming. Its nickname, the Dog Star, gave us the phrase dog days for the hottest part of summer. In 1844 Friedrich Bessel saw that its path across the sky wobbled, and he worked out that a hidden partner must pull on it. Alvan Graham Clark first saw Sirius B on 31 January 1862, while testing a new 18.5 inch telescope lens. In 1915 Walter Adams studied its light with the 60 inch telescope at Mount Wilson, and Sirius B became only the second white dwarf known. Sirius was also the first star whose speed towards or away from us was measured, by William Huggins in 1868.\n\nSirius is slowly getting closer to us, so it will grow a little brighter during the coming 60,000 years. In about the year 66,270 it will act as a pole star for the south. It should stay the brightest star in our sky for about 210,000 years, until Vega takes over. Sirius A will one day run out of hydrogen in its core and end as a white dwarf too.", photos: [{"src": "photos/sirius.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Sirius_A_and_B_Hubble_photo.jpg", "alt": "A Hubble picture of Sirius A as a bright blue-white glare with light spikes, and its partner Sirius B as a tiny dot to the lower left.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, H. Bond and M. Barstow / Hubble, public domain", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/sirius-toy.webp", "alt": "Sirius as this toy draws it", "credit": "Sirius in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/sirius-2.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d6/Sirius_A_%26_B_X-ray.jpg", "alt": "A Chandra X-ray image of the Sirius system: the white dwarf Sirius B is the bright source in the middle, Sirius A a fainter dot beside it, with spikes from the telescope’s grating.", "credit": "NASA/SAO/CXC / Chandra, public domain", "after": 2}] },
  procyon: { text: "Procyon shines brighter than any other star in Canis Minor, the Little Dog, and it usually ranks eighth among all the stars of the night sky. It sits only about 11.5 light years from us, so it counts as one of our close neighbours in space. With Sirius and Betelgeuse it makes the Winter Triangle, a large shape that is easy to spot on winter evenings in the north.\n\nWhat looks like a single point of light is really two stars. Procyon A, the bright one, is a white star with a surface near 6,580 K. It holds about 1.5 times the Sun’s mass, is twice as wide, and sends out about seven times as much light. Its partner, Procyon B, is a dim white dwarf: the small, dense core left over after a star has died. The two go around each other once in nearly 41 years, on a stretched path that brings them from 8.9 to 21 AU apart.\n\nProcyon A is nearing the close of its calm middle age. It has burned most of the hydrogen in its centre and is starting to grow. Over the next 10 to 100 million years it should swell until it is 80 to 150 times wider than today, and turn orange or red. Its surface also quivers in slow ripples, like the Sun’s. In 2004 Canada’s MOST space telescope watched it for 32 days and saw none, which puzzled astronomers. Later measurements from the ground, and from MOST itself in 2007, did find them.\n\nThe partner was suspected long before anyone saw it. Friedrich Bessel guessed in 1844 that an unseen body was tugging at Procyon, and Arthur Auwers worked out its orbit in 1862. In 1896 John Martin Schaeberle finally spotted it with the big 36 inch telescope at Lick Observatory. Procyon B is cooler than Sirius B, about 7,740 K. It holds only about 0.6 M☉, yet it is larger, some 8,600 km in radius against 5,800 km for Sirius B, a quirk of the crushed matter inside white dwarfs. It began as a star of about 2.6 M☉ and died some 1.2 billion years ago.\n\nThe name is Greek for \"before the dog\". From most northern places Procyon climbs above the horizon ahead of Sirius, the Dog Star. Many peoples have stories about it, and it is even shown on the Brazilian flag.", photos: [{"src": "photos/procyon.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Procyon_A_and_Procyon_B_%2829468251154%29.jpg/960px-Procyon_A_and_Procyon_B_%2829468251154%29.jpg", "alt": "A telescope photo of bright Procyon A with its faint white dwarf partner, Procyon B, close beside it.", "credit": "Giuseppe Donatiello, CC0", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/procyon-toy.webp", "alt": "Procyon as this toy draws it", "credit": "Procyon in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/procyon-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Hubble_heic0206j.jpg/960px-Hubble_heic0206j.jpg", "alt": "A wide photo of the winter sky: Procyon at upper left, orange Betelgeuse at upper right, the stars of Orion at right and bright Sirius at the bottom.", "credit": "Akira Fujii / ESA/Hubble, public domain", "after": 0}] },
  przyb: { text: "Przybylski's star, also called HD 101065, lies about 356 light years away in Centaurus. Too faint for the eye, it shines at magnitude 8. Antoni Przybylski found in 1961 that its light did not fit any normal star. Most stars show strong lines of iron. This one shows weak iron and strong lines of rare earth metals such as holmium and dysprosium.\n\nIt is a chemically peculiar star, about 1.5 times the Sun's mass and twice its width. A strong magnetic field and a quiet surface let heavy atoms float up and gather in patches. Some studies claim lines of short-lived elements that should have decayed long ago, which is why people call it the strangest star known. It also rings like a bell: its light pulses every 12 minutes, and it may take near 200 years to turn once." },
  sun: { text: "Our Sun is the star at the heart of the Solar System. This giant ball of plasma, a hot glowing gas, shines because hydrogen fuses into helium deep inside. Every second its core fuses about 600 billion kg of hydrogen, and about 4 billion kg of matter turns into energy. It is about 150 million km from Earth, so sunlight needs about 8 minutes to get here.\n\nNothing else in the Solar System comes close to it in size or weight. It is about 1.39 million km across, roughly 109 times as wide as Earth, and holds about 99.86% of the Solar System’s mass. The surface is about 5,800 K and the core about 15.7 million K. The thin outer layer, the corona, is far hotter than the surface, at 1 to 2 million K.\n\nLight made in the core takes a long road out. Dense gas soaks it up and gives it out again so many times that it needs between 10,000 and 170,000 years to reach the surface. Neutrinos, tiny particles that hardly touch matter, escape in about 2.3 seconds. For years detectors caught only a third of the neutrinos expected; in 2001 scientists found that the missing ones change type on the way. In the outer part of the Sun, hot gas rises and cool gas sinks, and these cells show on the surface as a pattern of bright grains called granulation.\n\nIt is a G2 V star, sometimes called a yellow dwarf. Its light is really white; it only looks yellow through our air. The Sun does not spin as one solid body: its equator turns about once every 25 days, but near the poles a turn takes about 33 days. Sunspots and flares come and go in a cycle of about 11 years.\n\nIt took shape some 4.6 billion years ago, when part of a huge cloud of dust and gas collapsed under its own weight. Most of it became the Sun, and the rest formed the planets. People have honoured the Sun as a god and studied it since ancient times. In 1868 Norman Lockyer found a new element in its light and named it helium, after Helios, the Greek Sun god.\n\nThe Sun is roughly halfway through its main life. In around 5 billion years its core will run out of hydrogen, and it will swell into a red giant, swallowing Mercury and Venus and perhaps Earth. Later it will throw off its outer gas as a glowing cloud called a planetary nebula. What is left will be a white dwarf, a dense core that slowly cools for trillions of years.", photos: [{"src": "photos/sun.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg/960px-The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg", "alt": "The Sun photographed in white light through a solar filter on 8 May 2019, with two small groups of dark sunspots and a darker edge.", "credit": "Matúš Motlo, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/sun-toy.webp", "alt": "The Sun as this toy draws it", "credit": "The Sun in this toy", "after": 4}, {"src": "photos/sun-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Highest_resolution_photo_of_Sun_%28NSF%29_as_of_January_20%2C_2020.jpg/960px-Highest_resolution_photo_of_Sun_%28NSF%29_as_of_January_20%2C_2020.jpg", "alt": "A close-up of the Sun’s surface from the Daniel K. Inouye Solar Telescope in 2020: bright cells of rising gas packed together, with dark lanes between them.", "credit": "NSO/AURA/NSF, CC BY 4.0", "after": 2}] },
  epseri: { text: "Epsilon Eridani is an orange star in Eridanus, the River, just below the celestial equator, so people on most of Earth can see it. It lies about 10.5 light years away. Of all the stars and star systems we can see without a telescope, only two are closer. Since 2015 it has also had a proper name, Ran, after a Norse goddess of the sea.\n\nIt is a little smaller and lighter than the Sun, with about 0.82 times the Sun’s mass and about three quarters of its width. It gives off about a third as much light, and its surface is near 5,000 K. It is also young: most estimates put its age between about 200 million and 1 billion years. Young stars are restless. It has strong magnetic activity and dark spots, and its wind of particles blows 30 times harder than the Sun’s. Its magnetic field is on average more than forty times stronger than the Sun’s at the surface, and its activity rises and falls in two cycles at once, of about 3 and 12.7 years. It turns once in about 11 days.\n\nAstronomers think a giant planet goes round it every 7.3 to 7.6 years. The planet, now called AEgir, was announced in 2000 and was argued about for nearly two decades before most experts accepted it. A team led by Artie Hatzes found signs of it by tracking tiny shifts in the star’s light from 1980 to 2000, and Hubble measured the star’s wobble from 2001 to 2003. Recent studies put its mass at 0.63 to 0.78 times that of Jupiter. Rings of dust circle the star too: a cold belt of comets about 70 au out, like our Kuiper belt, and warmer dust from about 3 to 20 au. The empty gap between them hints at more planets, though images from the James Webb Space Telescope rule out any planet heavier than Saturn beyond 16 au.\n\nPtolemy listed the star in his catalogue in the 2nd century AD. Between 1881 and 1883 William Elkin measured how far away it is from the Royal Observatory at the Cape of Good Hope.\n\nFrank Drake’s Project Ozma pointed the Tatel radio telescope at it and listened at 1,420 MHz, the natural radio note of hydrogen, for signals from aliens, but heard nothing. A second try in 2010 found nothing either.\n\nIn about 31,500 years a nearby pair of stars, Luyten 726-8, will pass within about 0.93 light years of it. Writers of science fiction love it, and some studies have named it as a goal for travel between the stars.", photos: [{"src": "photos/epseri.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Epsilon_Eridani_ALMA_Image.png/960px-Epsilon_Eridani_ALMA_Image.png", "alt": "A radio telescope image of Epsilon Eridani as a dot at the centre of a wide, oval ring of cold dust.", "credit": "Mark Booth / ALMA, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 2}, {"src": "photos/epseri-toy.webp", "alt": "Epsilon Eridani as this toy draws it", "credit": "Epsilon Eridani in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/epseri-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/GBTatelDrake.jpg/960px-GBTatelDrake.jpg", "alt": "Frank Drake standing in a grassy field in front of the large Tatel radio telescope dish at Green Bank, the telescope he used to listen to Epsilon Eridani.", "credit": "NRAO/AUI/NSF, CC BY 4.0", "after": 4}] },
  proxima: { text: "Apart from the Sun, Proxima Centauri is the star nearest to us. It lies about 4.25 light years away, in Centaurus, a constellation of the southern sky. Its name is Latin and means the nearest star of Centaurus. It belongs to the Alpha Centauri family of stars, so it is also called Alpha Centauri C.\n\nProxima is a red dwarf, a small and cool star. It has only about one eighth of the Sun’s mass and is about one seventh as wide. It is packed tight, though: on average it is about 33 times denser than the Sun. More than 85% of its power leaves as infrared, the heat light our eyes cannot see. It is far too dim to see with the eye alone. You need a telescope at least 8 cm across to spot it.\n\nIt is also a flare star, as Harlow Shapley announced in 1951. Proxima is so small that its whole inside churns, like boiling water. This churning, called convection, drives a strong magnetic field, which now and then sets off sudden bursts that make the star much brighter for a short time. In 2016 one huge flare made it about 68 times brighter than normal. The strongest flares reach about 27 million K, which is hot enough to give off X-rays.\n\nRobert Innes, a Scottish astronomer working in South Africa, found the star in 1915. He noticed that it moved across the sky in step with Alpha Centauri. Today we know of two planets around Proxima, and a third that is still in doubt. One of them, Proxima b, lies in the zone where liquid water could exist, and it goes around the star in about 11 days. In 2016 the Pale Red Dot team of 31 scientists, led by Guillem Anglada-Escudé, confirmed it. They measured how the star sways to and fro as the planet tugs on it, using the HARPS and UVES spectrographs. Whether life could survive the flares there is still unclear.\n\nProxima circles Alpha Centauri A and B once in about 547,000 years, and today it is near the far end of that orbit, nearly 13,000 AU away. It has held the title of nearest star for some 32,000 years and should keep it for about 25,000 more. The churning stirs the helium ash all through the star, so Proxima will burn nearly all of its hydrogen, where the Sun will use only about a tenth. It will keep shining for about four trillion more years. It will never swell into a red giant. Instead it will slowly become a white dwarf made mostly of helium, a dim leftover core that cools over time.", photos: [{"src": "photos/proxima.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/95/New_shot_of_Proxima_Centauri%2C_our_nearest_neighbour.jpg", "alt": "A Hubble picture of Proxima Centauri shining brightly with four light spikes, among many faint background stars.", "credit": "ESA/Hubble & NASA, CC BY 4.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/proxima-toy.webp", "alt": "Proxima Centauri as this toy draws it", "credit": "Proxima Centauri in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/proxima-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Alpha%2C_Beta_and_Proxima_Centauri_%281%29.jpg/960px-Alpha%2C_Beta_and_Proxima_Centauri_%281%29.jpg", "alt": "A wide night-sky photo with Alpha Centauri shining brightly; a small red circle marks faint Proxima Centauri below it among many Milky Way stars.", "credit": "Skatebiker, CC BY-SA 3.0", "after": 4}] },
  luhman: { text: "Luhman 16 is a pair of brown dwarfs in Vela, the Sails, a constellation of the southern sky. A brown dwarf is bigger than a planet but too small to burn hydrogen the way a true star does. At about 6.5 light years, this pair is the third nearest system to us, behind Alpha Centauri and Barnard’s Star, and the closest brown dwarfs we know.\n\nThe two hold about 35 and 29 times the mass of Jupiter. They circle each other roughly three and a half times as far apart as Earth is from the Sun, and one lap takes about 27 years. Each spins fast: Luhman 16A turns in about 6.9 hours and Luhman 16B in about 5.3 hours.\n\nTheir weather is wild. The second dwarf, Luhman 16B, is among the most changeable brown dwarfs found so far; its light can swing by more than a fifth. On 5 May 2013 Ian Crossfield’s team watched it for five hours, one full turn, with ESO’s Very Large Telescope, and made the first weather map of a brown dwarf. It shows a large dark patch at middle latitudes and a bright area near the pole. Dark parts are thick clouds; bright parts are holes where hotter light from deep inside shines through. Later studies found strong winds forming stripes along the equator, much as on Jupiter, and gases such as water vapour, methane and ammonia. In 2024 the James Webb Space Telescope found tiny grains of silicate, the stuff of rock and sand, in clouds high in the air of Luhman 16A.\n\nKevin Luhman, an astronomer at Penn State, announced the find in 2013. He compared infrared pictures taken at different times by NASA’s WISE space telescope and looked for anything that moved quickly. Earlier searches had missed the pair because it lies close to the crowded band of the Milky Way, where faint objects are hard to pick out. The Gemini South telescope in Chile then split it into two dwarfs only 1.5 arcseconds apart. Eric Mamajek suggested the short name, since the catalogue label runs to 24 characters.\n\nThe pair is about 400 to 800 million years old, which fits its place in the Oceanus moving group, a family of stars about 510 million years old that travel together. Around 36,000 years ago it passed the Sun at roughly 5 light years. A possible planet was reported in 2013, but later checks with Hubble and other telescopes found no sign of one.", photos: [{"src": "photos/luhman.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Two_Brown_Dwarfs_in_Our_Backyard.jpg/960px-Two_Brown_Dwarfs_in_Our_Backyard.jpg", "alt": "An infrared WISE view of the star field with Luhman 16 at its centre, and a Gemini telescope close-up that splits it into two brown dwarfs.", "credit": "NASA/JPL-Caltech, Gemini Observatory, public domain", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/luhman-toy.webp", "alt": "Luhman 16 as this toy draws it", "credit": "Luhman 16 in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/luhman-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Surface_map_of_Luhman_16B_recreated_from_VLT_observations.jpg/960px-Surface_map_of_Luhman_16B_recreated_from_VLT_observations.jpg", "alt": "Six globes in a ring showing the weather map of Luhman 16B from the Very Large Telescope as it turns: orange with darker and brighter cloud patches.", "credit": "ESO/I. Crossfield, CC BY 4.0", "after": 2}] },
  wise: { text: "WISE 0855−0714 is a brown dwarf, an object too light to shine by fusion like a true star. It lies about 7.4 light years away in the constellation Hydra. Only three star systems are closer to the Sun, so it is the fourth nearest.\n\nNo colder brown dwarf is known. Its temperature is about 276 K, or 3 °C, close to room temperature. It is about as wide as Jupiter but holds only 3 to 10 times Jupiter’s mass. A brown dwarf only cools as it ages, and a lighter one cools faster, so the mass follows from its temperature and an assumed age of 1 to 10 billion years. That is so light that it could count as a planet drifting alone in space, though research papers still class it among brown dwarfs. In 2024 Webb found deuterium, a heavy form of hydrogen, in its methane; a heavier body would have burned it up.\n\nBecause it is so cold, it gives off almost no visible light. It glows mainly in infrared, the heat light our eyes cannot see, so infrared telescopes, like the James Webb Space Telescope and WISE, see it best. Webb found water vapour, methane, ammonia and carbon monoxide in its air. High up there is less water vapour than expected: the water condenses into grains that sink to deeper layers, a kind of rain. In 2026 a team found patchy clouds of water ice there.\n\nThe WISE space telescope first took its picture on 4 May 2010 while mapping the whole sky. In March 2013 Kevin Luhman, searching for unseen partners of the Sun, noticed that it moved across the sky very fast, a sign that it is close. Only two stars, Barnard’s Star and Kapteyn’s Star, move faster across our sky. Luhman checked it with the Spitzer and Gemini North telescopes, and NASA announced the find in April 2014.\n\nThe Spitzer Space Telescope saw its brightness change a little, by 4 to 5%, which suggests it turns once every 9.7 to 14 hours. Webb also watched it for 11 hours to look for moons passing in front of it, but found none. A moon around double the size of Titan, a moon of Saturn, would most likely have shown up.", photos: [{"src": "photos/wise.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/WISE_0855-0714_NIRCam_Movement.jpg/960px-WISE_0855-0714_NIRCam_Movement.jpg", "alt": "Two infrared JWST pictures taken about five months apart, in which the orange point of WISE 0855−0714 has moved against the background galaxies.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA / JWST, Alves de Oliveira et al., processed by Melina Thévenot, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/wise-toy.webp", "alt": "WISE 0855−0714 as this toy draws it", "credit": "WISE 0855−0714 in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/wise-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/WISE_0855_%28gemini1605a%29.jpg/960px-WISE_0855_%28gemini1605a%29.jpg", "alt": "An artist’s impression of WISE 0855−0714 as a dim red-brown ball with dark cloud bands, against a starry background.", "credit": "Artist’s impression, International Gemini Observatory/Joy Pollard, CC BY 4.0", "after": 2}] },
  ttauri: { text: "T Tauri is a young star system in Taurus, the Bull, and it changes in brightness. From Earth it seems to sit among the Hyades, a star cluster, but it is really about 318 light years farther away. It gave its name to a whole class of baby stars, the T Tauri stars, even though it is an odd example of them.\n\nThere are three stars. The brightest, T Tau N, has about 2.1 to 2.4 times the Sun’s mass. Roughly 300 AU away is a southern pair. T Tau Sa has about 2 to 2.3 times the Sun’s mass, and T Tau Sb about 0.4 to 0.5. These two are only about 7 AU apart and circle each other in about 27 years. How long T Tau N takes to circle the pair is still poorly known: estimates run from 400 to 14,000 years.\n\nStars this young do not yet fuse hydrogen in their cores. They glow with heat left over from their collapse, and they still pull in gas from discs around them, so their light flickers over weeks or months. Fast jets of gas stream out. Magnetic fields in the discs launch these jets, and the jets carry away spin; without them a young star could not grow beyond about 0.05 M☉. A ring of dust around the southern pair blocks its visible light, so we see that pair mainly in infrared. The disc of T Tau N faces us almost head on, so that star shows clearly in visible light.\n\nJohn Russell Hind found T Tauri in October 1852, together with a nearby cloud now called Hind’s Variable Nebula. That cloud fades and comes back. It shines by reflected starlight from T Tauri, and it dims when material drifts between the star and the cloud.\n\nIn 1890 Sherburne Burnham turned the new 36 inch Lick refractor to look for Hind’s nebula, which had been missing on and off since the 1860s. He spotted a small glowing patch right next to the star instead. It was the first Herbig-Haro object ever found: a bright knot made where a jet crashes into cold gas nearby.\n\nThe system is still changing. Between 2015 and 2024 T Tau N faded by about 2 magnitudes, likely because the dust ring of the southern pair is moving in front of it. The dimming may go on for 60 to 70 years, perhaps longer, and the star may vanish from view in visible light. A gap in the disc around T Tau N hints at a planet about as heavy as Saturn.", photos: [{"src": "photos/ttauri.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bf/T_Tauri_Hubble_UV.jpg", "alt": "A Hubble ultraviolet image of the T Tauri system, mapping the glow of molecular hydrogen around the young stars.", "credit": "NASA/ESA Hubble, A. Brown et al., M. Thévenot, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/ttauri-toy.webp", "alt": "T Tauri as this toy draws it", "credit": "T Tauri in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/ttauri-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/N1555s.jpg/960px-N1555s.jpg", "alt": "A telescope photo of T Tauri as a bright star with spikes, beside the glowing orange and brown cloud of Hind’s Variable Nebula and dark dust.", "credit": "Adam Block/Mount Lemmon SkyCenter/University of Arizona, CC BY-SA 3.0", "after": 4}] },
  fomalhaut: { text: "No star in Piscis Austrinus, the Southern Fish, outshines Fomalhaut, and it ranks high among the brightest stars we see at night. It is about 25 light years away from us. It is best seen from the southern half of the Earth, but people in much of the north can see it too, low in the sky in autumn.\n\nFomalhaut is a young white star, about 440 million years old. It has nearly twice the Sun’s mass and is about 1.84 times as wide. It gives out about 16 times as much light as the Sun, and its surface is about 8,600 K.\n\nWhat makes Fomalhaut special is the dust around it. Several rings of dust and rock circle the star. The outer ring lies about 133 AU out, which is 133 times the distance from the Earth to the Sun, and it has a sharp inner edge. Starlight should blow such fine dust out of the system quickly, so something must keep making it. Images from the Herschel space telescope, studied in 2012, showed fluffy grains like comet dust: comets about a kilometre across crash into each other, perhaps about 2,000 of them each day. New Scientist compared it to the Great Eye of Sauron.\n\nIn 2008 the Hubble Space Telescope took pictures of what seemed to be a planet just inside the outer ring. Named Fomalhaut b, it was the first planet candidate around another star seen in visible light. Later studies, in 2019 and 2023, found that it was growing and fading. It now looks like a spreading cloud of dust from a big crash between rocky bodies, not a planet.\n\nIn 2022 the James Webb Space Telescope looked at the rings in mid-infrared light with its MIRI camera, and could not make out Fomalhaut b. Closer to the star there are more discs: a fine, carbon-rich ash about 0.1 AU out, and a disc of larger grains beyond it. In December 2025 astronomers reported another big crash in the system, much like the one that made Fomalhaut b.\n\nFomalhaut has two far away partners, the red dwarf LP 876-10 and the smaller star TW Piscis Austrini. They are spread over about 8 degrees of sky, yet all three move through space together. In 2013 the Herschel telescope found a cold dust disc around LP 876-10 too; systems of several stars with several dust discs are very rare. Its name comes from Arabic words for the mouth of the fish, and the Persians counted it as one of their four royal stars.", photos: [{"src": "photos/fomalhaut.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Fomalhaut_B_entire-Hubble_Telescope.jpg/960px-Fomalhaut_B_entire-Hubble_Telescope.jpg", "alt": "A Hubble picture of the oval ring of dust around Fomalhaut, glowing orange, with the glare of the star itself blocked out at the centre.", "credit": "NASA / Hubble, public domain", "after": 2}, {"src": "photos/fomalhaut-toy.webp", "alt": "Fomalhaut as this toy draws it", "credit": "Fomalhaut in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/fomalhaut-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Fomalhaut_Dusty_Debris_Disk_%28MIRI_Image%29.png/960px-Fomalhaut_Dusty_Debris_Disk_%28MIRI_Image%29.png", "alt": "A James Webb infrared image of the dust around Fomalhaut: an orange outer ring tilted into an oval, with fainter inner belts and a dark spot where the star is masked.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA, A. Gáspár, A. Pagan, public domain", "after": 4}] },
  arcturus: { text: "Arcturus leads the constellation Boötes, the Herdsman, and outshines every other star in the northern half of the sky. Only Sirius, Canopus and Alpha Centauri look brighter, so it ranks fourth in the whole night sky. Its distance is about 36.7 light years, and it is the second nearest giant star, after Pollux. A simple trick for finding it is to follow the curve of the Big Dipper’s handle: \"arc to Arcturus\".\n\nArcturus belongs to the red giants: it is an old star that has run out of hydrogen in its centre and puffed up. It is about 25 times as wide as the Sun, around 35 million km, yet its mass is close to the Sun’s, about 1.1 times as much. In total it gives off some 170 to 180 times as much energy as the Sun, much of it as infrared light, because its surface is cooler than ours.\n\nIts age is about 7.1 billion years, older than the Sun. It contains only around a third as much iron and other heavy elements as the Sun, and astronomers count it among the old stars of the galaxy’s disc. They read this from its spectrum, the starlight split into a rainbow, where each element leaves its own dark lines. As the brightest K-type giant in the sky, a class of orange giants, it has been mapped in several detailed spectrum atlases. It rushes past us at about 122 km per second, and it travels with a loose group of about 52 similar old stars, called the Arcturus stream. It is now almost at its closest to the Sun, which it will reach in about 4,000 years.\n\nPeople have watched it since ancient times. Ptolemy noted its slightly red colour. In 1635 Jean-Baptiste Morin looked at it through a telescope in daylight, the first time any star except the Sun had been seen in full day. The name is ancient Greek for \"Guardian of the Bear\".\n\nArcturus is still growing as a giant. Later it should light helium in its core in a sudden event called the helium flash. Hints of a hidden partner or planet have come up, but none has been confirmed. In 1993 its speed seemed to swing slowly, as if a body about 12 times Jupiter’s mass were pulling it, but Aldebaran and Pollux showed the same swing, so the star itself is the likely cause.", photos: [{"src": "photos/arcturus.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Arcturus_%28optical%29.png", "alt": "An optical sky survey image of Arcturus, a bright star glowing among faint background stars.", "credit": "MAST, STScI / NASA, public domain", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/arcturus-toy.webp", "alt": "Arcturus as this toy draws it", "credit": "Arcturus in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/arcturus-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/High_resolution_spectrum_of_Arcturus_%28noao-arct%29.jpg/960px-High_resolution_spectrum_of_Arcturus_%28noao-arct%29.jpg", "alt": "The spectrum of Arcturus cut into many strips stacked from red to violet, crossed by thousands of dark lines from the elements in its atmosphere.", "credit": "N.A. Sharp/NOIRLab/NSF/AURA, CC BY 4.0", "after": 2}] },
  betalyr: { text: "Beta Lyrae, also called Sheliak, is a group of stars in the constellation Lyra. It lies about 960 light years away. Without a telescope it looks like one point of light, but it holds six stars. The bright main part is itself a close pair that eclipse each other, plus a possible third star, found in 2006. The next brightest member, Beta Lyrae B, shines at magnitude 7.2 and is easy to see with binoculars.\n\nThe two stars of the close pair orbit each other every 12.9 days. Seen from Earth, their orbit is almost edge on, so each star passes in front of the other in turn. Because of this, the system’s brightness rises and falls between magnitude 3.2 and 4.4.\n\nThe pair are so close that they share material. The star that is now lighter was once the heavier one. It aged faster and swelled into a giant. Each star rules a small zone around it where its own gravity holds the gas; the giant grew past its zone, and most of its gas spilled onto its partner. Today the gainer has about 13 times the Sun’s mass and the giver about 3. The giver is now about 15 times as wide as the Sun, with a surface near 13,300 K. The gas still flows, enough to build a whole Sun in 50,000 years, and that makes the orbit about 19 seconds longer each year.\n\nThe gainer star hides inside a thick disk of gas, and jets stick out above and below it. The disk blocks our view of that star, so its exact type is hard to pin down. The disk itself gives about a fifth of the light of the whole system, and it adds bright lines to the spectrum. On top of the eclipses there are smaller, slower changes in brightness, roughly every 282 days, which are thought to come from changes in the disk.\n\nIn 1784 John Goodricke, an amateur astronomer from Britain, found that its light changes. In 1894 Aristarkh Belopolsky showed that it is an eclipsing pair. In 2008 the CHARA Array, a group of linked telescopes, made the first picture of the giver star and the disk in near infrared light, and the orbit could be worked out for the first time. Beta Lyrae became the model for a whole class of eclipsing stars. It sits in a loose cluster of roughly 100 stars called Gaia 8, thought to be 30 to 100 million years old.", photos: [{"src": "photos/betalyr.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_a_vampire_star_and_its_victim.jpg/960px-Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_a_vampire_star_and_its_victim.jpg", "alt": "An artist’s impression of a star pulling gas off its partner; it shows the same kind of pair, not Beta Lyrae itself", "credit": "Artist’s impression, ESO/M. Kornmesser/S.E. de Mink, CC BY 4.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/betalyr-toy.webp", "alt": "Beta Lyrae as this toy draws it", "credit": "Beta Lyrae in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/betalyr-2.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Goodricke_John.jpg", "alt": "A portrait of the young astronomer John Goodricke in a blue coat, beside a large globe.", "credit": "James Scouler, public domain", "after": 4}] },
  s5hvs1: { text: "S5-HVS1 is a star in Grus, the Crane, a constellation of the southern sky. It lies about 29,000 light years away. It is a main-sequence star of type A: a hot, white star that is still burning hydrogen in its core. It holds about 2.35 times the Sun’s mass.\n\nWhat makes it special is its speed. It moves at about 1,755 km per second, almost 0.6 percent of light’s speed, or about 6 million km each hour. When it was found in 2019, no faster star of its kind was known. Its spectrum, taken with the 3.9 metre Anglo-Australian Telescope in New South Wales, gave its speed away from us, and the Gaia space telescope added its drift across the sky. At that speed the pull of the Milky Way cannot hold it, and it is on its way out of our galaxy for good.\n\nAstronomers think it was thrown out by Sagittarius A*, the giant black hole in the middle of the Milky Way. Traced backwards, its path points straight to the centre of the galaxy. It left there about 4.8 million years ago at about 1,800 km each second. A likely story is that the star once had a partner, and the two stars came too close to the black hole. Its pull tore the pair apart: the black hole captured the partner, and S5-HVS1 was flung outward at huge speed. This idea is called the Hills mechanism, after the astronomer Jack Hills, who proposed it. If it is right, S5-HVS1 is the first star known to have been ejected this way.\n\nThe star was found by Sergey Koposov of Carnegie Mellon University and his team. They were working on the Southern Stellar Stream Spectroscopic Survey, called S5 for short, which gave the star the first part of its name. S5-HVS1 turned up by chance, in spare time the survey gave to other targets. HVS1 stands for hypervelocity star, a term for stars that move fast enough to escape the galaxy. It also has an older catalogue name, HE 2251-5127.\n\nIts trip links two very different places. It began deep in the crowded centre of the galaxy, right beside the black hole, and it is now out in the quiet southern sky, heading into the empty space between galaxies.", photos: [{"src": "photos/s5hvs1.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Runaway_Stars_Reveal_Hidden_Black_Hole_In_Milky_Way%E2%80%99s_Nearest_Neighbor_%28Artist%27s_impression%29.jpg/960px-Runaway_Stars_Reveal_Hidden_Black_Hole_In_Milky_Way%E2%80%99s_Nearest_Neighbor_%28Artist%27s_impression%29.jpg", "alt": "An artist’s impression of a star flung out after a black hole tore its pair apart; it shows the same kind of event, not S5-HVS1 itself", "credit": "Artist’s impression, CfA/Melissa Weiss, CC0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/s5hvs1-toy.webp", "alt": "S5-HVS1 as this toy draws it", "credit": "S5-HVS1 in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/s5hvs1-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Anglo-Australian_Telescope_lower_deck.jpg/960px-Anglo-Australian_Telescope_lower_deck.jpg", "alt": "The Anglo-Australian Telescope inside its dome: a large white telescope tube in a green horseshoe mount, seen from below.", "credit": "Diceman Stephen West, CC BY-SA 3.0", "after": 1}] },
  rigel: { text: "Rigel is a huge, hot, blue star of the kind called a supergiant. It sits in the constellation Orion, where it marks one of the hunter’s feet. Most of the time it outshines every other star in Orion, and it is the seventh brightest in the night sky. It lies about 850 light years from us. Its light falls on nearby clouds such as the Witch Head Nebula, IC 2118, a reflection nebula: dust that shines by scattering Rigel’s light.\n\nRigel is roughly 74 to 79 times as wide as the Sun and about 21 times as heavy, and its surface is about 12,100 K. The figure of 74 comes from the Navy Precision Optical Interferometer, which in 2018 measured how wide its disc looks. Guesses of how much light it gives out vary a lot, from about 61,500 to 363,000 times the Sun’s, because its distance is hard to pin down. A strong stellar wind, a stream of gas flowing off its surface, carries matter away from it. Rigel loses about 1.5 ten-millionths of the Sun’s mass each year, ten million times as fast as the Sun. It was born with about 24 times the Sun’s mass and has lost about three since.\n\nRigel looks like one point of light, but it is a group of four or more stars. William Herschel saw its faint companion in 1781, and Sherburne Wesley Burnham split that companion in two in 1878. Close beside it sits a much fainter set of three blue-white stars, each with three to four times the Sun’s mass. Two of them orbit one another in 10 days, and the third circles that pair every 63 years. Rigel and the set of three take about 24,000 years to go around each other.\n\nThe name Rigel comes from Arabic words for the left foot of Jauzah, an old Arabic name for Orion. The first known written record of the name dates from 1521. For the Māori of New Zealand the star is Puanga, and its rising before dawn can signal that their new year is beginning.\n\nRigel is only about 8 million years old, yet it has already used up the hydrogen in its core and swollen into a supergiant. Its brightness changes a little as its outer layers pulse. It shakes in at least 19 different ways at once, its surface rippling rather than swelling evenly, with periods from about 1.2 to 74 days. One day it will blow up as a supernova. Seen from Earth, that blast could shine about as bright as a quarter Moon. It would leave behind a black hole, or else a neutron star.", photos: [{"src": "photos/rigel.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/89/RigelAandB.jpg", "alt": "Rigel as a bright white dot in a small telescope, with its much fainter companion just below and to the right.", "credit": "NVN271, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 2}, {"src": "photos/rigel-toy.webp", "alt": "Rigel as this toy draws it", "credit": "Rigel in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/rigel-2.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4a/Treasures3.jpg", "alt": "Rigel as a bright blue-white glow at lower left, with the Witch Head Nebula, a faint blue cloud shaped like a face in profile, at upper right.", "credit": "Robert Gendler, CC BY 4.0", "after": 0}] },
  hubblev1: { text: "Hubble V1 is a pulsing star in the Andromeda Galaxy, about 2.5 million light years away. It is a Cepheid: a yellow supergiant that swells and shrinks, and brightens and fades, on a fixed beat, here every 31.4 days.\n\nIn October 1923 Edwin Hubble found it on a photographic plate from the Mount Wilson telescope. He crossed out the N he had written for nova and wrote VAR! beside it. A Cepheid's beat tells its true brightness, so its faint light gave its distance: far outside the Milky Way. Andromeda was a galaxy of its own, and the universe was much larger than people had thought." },
  gaiabh1: { text: "Gaia BH1 is a star much like the Sun, about 1,560 light years away in Ophiuchus. Every 186 days it circles something it cannot see: a black hole of about 9.6 suns, as far from it as Mars is from the Sun.\n\nNo gas falls into this black hole, so it gives off no light and no X-rays. Astronomers found it in 2022 from the wobble of the star alone, measured by the Gaia space telescope. It is the nearest black hole known. Here, pulled back, the hole shows only as it truly would: it bends the light of the stars behind it into a small dark lens." },
  etacar: { text: "Eta Carinae is a pair of very heavy stars about 7,500 light years away in Carina, the Keel. The larger one holds about 100 times the Sun's mass, among the heaviest stars known, and shines millions of times brighter than the Sun. It is a luminous blue variable: an unstable star close to the limit where its own light pushes it apart.\n\nIn the 1840s it had the Great Eruption. For a while it was the second brightest star in the night sky, then it faded from view. In those years it blew off about ten suns of gas and dust. That cloud, the Homunculus Nebula, still flies outward at about 650 km/s: two round lobes on either side of the star and a thin skirt of streaks round its waist. In Hubble's pictures the dust shines tan and cream in the light of the hidden star.\n\nThe smaller star circles the larger one every 5.54 years, and their winds clash between them. Eta Carinae will probably end as a supernova, perhaps soon in the life of a star." },
  betelgeuse: { text: "Betelgeuse is the reddish star at the upper left of Orion. Most nights it ranks tenth among the brightest stars, and in Orion only Rigel beats it. Its distance is uncertain, most likely about 400 to 600 light years. Its colour has been noted since ancient times: Ptolemy called it orange-tawny, though Chinese astronomers three centuries before him recorded it as yellow.\n\nIt is a red supergiant, one of the largest stars we can see. Its radius is between about 640 and 764 times the Sun’s. Put in place of the Sun, it would reach past the asteroid belt and swallow the paths of all four inner planets, Earth included. It holds roughly 10 to 20 times the Sun’s mass and is younger than 10 million years. Its brightness wobbles between magnitude 0.0 and 1.6, a wider swing than any other star this bright, and it turns only once in about 36 years.\n\nIn 1920 Albert Michelson and Francis Pease measured how big it looks, a first for any star beyond the Sun. They fixed a six metre interferometer, a beam that joins light from two mirrors, to the front of the 2.5 metre telescope at Mount Wilson Observatory. Hubble then took, in 1995, the first ordinary picture of the disc of another star. Its surface is far from smooth. Giant convection cells, bubbles of hot gas rising from inside, are a large part of the star’s width, and each hot spot they make can give out 5 to 10 percent of its light. In 2026 ALMA, a large array of radio dishes in Chile, showed this lumpy surface in detail.\n\nBetelgeuse is also a runaway: it was thrown out of a group of stars that includes Orion’s Belt and now ploughs through space at 30 km per second, pushing up a curved wave of gas more than four light years across. That bow shock was first imaged in 1997. The star is also known for its Great Dimming of 2019 to 2020. A small partner, named Siwarha in 2025, probably circles close to it, about 8.6 times as far from it as Earth is from the Sun, once in about six years. Gemini North reported a likely sighting in 2025, and in 2026 the Very Large Telescope picked it up again.\n\nOne day its core will collapse and it will explode as a supernova, probably sometime in the coming 100,000 years. For over three months it would shine about as bright as a half Moon, easy to see in daylight, but life on Earth would be safe. A neutron star of about 1.5 times the Sun’s mass would probably remain.", photos: [{"src": "photos/betelgeuse.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/ESO-Betelgeuse.jpg/960px-ESO-Betelgeuse.jpg", "alt": "A sharp infrared image from the Very Large Telescope of Betelgeuse’s disc, with a plume of gas reaching out from the star.", "credit": "ESO/P. Kervella, CC BY 4.0", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/betelgeuse-toy.webp", "alt": "Betelgeuse as this toy draws it", "credit": "Betelgeuse in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/betelgeuse-2.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/84/A_bubbling_Betelgeuse_%28potw2634a%29.jpg", "alt": "ALMA image of Betelgeuse’s surface: a lumpy orange disc with a bright hot spot near its top.", "credit": "ESO/W. Dent et al., CC BY 4.0", "after": 2}] },
  vycma: { text: "VY Canis Majoris, a red hypergiant, is among the largest stars known. It sits in Canis Major, about 3,700 to 3,900 light years from us. Radio telescopes measured that distance in 2008 and 2012 by tracking masers around the star, gas clouds that boost microwaves like a natural laser. It also ranks with the most luminous stars of our galaxy, the Milky Way.\n\nIts radius is about 1,420 times the Sun’s. If it took the Sun’s place, it would stretch past the orbit of Jupiter. Light would need 6 hours to go once around its surface, while for the Sun it needs only 14.5 seconds. It has about 17 times the Sun’s mass, give or take 8, and recent estimates say it gives off about 180,000 to 270,000 times as much light as the Sun.\n\nThe star is losing mass very fast. Huge bursts of gas, a bit like the outbursts on the Sun but far larger, have thrown out arcs, knots and jets. Together they built a big, lumpy cloud of gas and dust around it. That dust hides much of its visible light. So even though it is so luminous, you need a telescope to see it, and from Earth it ranges between magnitude 6.5 and 9.6.\n\nIt sheds about 5.6 ten-thousandths of the Sun’s mass each year, one of the highest rates known. Hubble and Keck images combined into a 3D map show that the arcs and knots formed at different times and that the jets point in random directions. The Very Large Telescope’s SPHERE instrument has also imaged the cloud close in, with the star hidden behind a dark disc.\n\nJérôme Lalande, a French astronomer, listed it in 1801 as a star of the 7th magnitude. Since 1847 people have called it a deep red, crimson star. In the 1800s observers thought they saw at least six stars there. These turned out to be bright patches in its cloud, a nebula bright enough to be found in 1917 with an 18 cm telescope. It has grown dimmer as seen from Earth since 1850.\n\nThe star is younger than 10 million years, about 8 million by one model, yet it is near the end of its life. Big stars burn out fast, and a star like this spends only about 100,000 to 500,000 years as a red hypergiant. It may blow up as a supernova, some time in the coming 100,000 years, or its core may fall straight in on itself. Either way, it will most likely leave a black hole.", photos: [{"src": "photos/vycma.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/04/VY_Canis_Majoris_Ejecta_Shell.jpg", "alt": "Hubble image of the lumpy cloud of gas and dust that VY Canis Majoris has thrown off, with the bright star near its centre.", "credit": "NASA, ESA / Hubble, processed by Judy Schmidt, CC BY 2.0", "after": 2}, {"src": "photos/vycma-toy.webp", "alt": "VY Canis Majoris as this toy draws it", "credit": "VY Canis Majoris in this toy", "after": 4}, {"src": "photos/vycma-2.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/VLT_image_of_the_surroundings_of_VY_Canis_Majoris_seen_with_SPHERE.jpg", "alt": "Very Large Telescope image of the glowing red and blue cloud around VY Canis Majoris; the star is hidden behind a dark disc, and the crossed lines are artefacts of the instrument.", "credit": "ESO, CC BY 4.0", "after": 3}] },
  st218: { text: "Stephenson 2-18, a huge red star also known as Stephenson 2 DFK 1, lies in Scutum, the Shield. It is a red supergiant, and maybe an even rarer red hypergiant. It is about 19,000 light years away, near a star cluster called Stephenson 2, in one of the spiral arms of the Milky Way. Most astronomers now count it as a member of that cluster.\n\nFew known stars are bigger. A 2026 study gives it a radius of about 1,840 times the Sun’s, so its volume is about 6.26 billion times larger. If it took the Sun’s place, its surface would reach past Jupiter’s orbit and nearly to Saturn’s. It gives off about 321,000 times as much light as the Sun, yet its surface is cool for a star, about 3,200 K, which is why it glows red.\n\nIts size has been hard to pin down. A 2013 study with the Australia Telescope Compact Array, a set of radio dishes, made it even bigger, about 2,150 times the Sun’s radius. That is larger than theory says a star like this can be: the limit is near 1,500 times the Sun’s radius, 3,500 K and 320,000 times the Sun’s light. A star that big could not stay steady. Because the distance may be wrong by up to half, the size is uncertain too.\n\nIt first entered a catalogue in 1988, as IRAS 18363-0607. In 1990 Charles Bruce Stephenson found the cluster in data from an infrared survey. The first guess put it about 98,000 light years away, far more than today’s value. The star was number 1 in the first study of the cluster’s stars and number 18 in a 2010 study, which explains its two names. For years astronomers argued about whether the star truly belongs to the cluster, since it moves a little differently from its neighbours. It moves away from us at about 89 km each second, about 20 less than the cluster. That gap is a typical speed for gas flowing off a red supergiant, so its expanding shell may simply mask its true motion.\n\nStephenson 2-18 is shedding its outer layers fast, about 1.35 hundred-thousandths of the Sun’s mass each year, one of the highest rates known for a red supergiant. Water masers, gas that boosts microwaves like a natural laser, glow around it, and ALMA has mapped the carbon monoxide there. It may be close to blowing off those layers and becoming a very hot star, such as a luminous blue variable or a Wolf-Rayet star.", photos: [{"src": "photos/st218.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Stephenson_2-18_zoomed_in%2C_2MASS_survey%2C_2003.png/960px-Stephenson_2-18_zoomed_in%2C_2MASS_survey%2C_2003.png", "alt": "An infrared sky image with Stephenson 2-18 as a bright orange star in the centre and its cluster at upper left.", "credit": "2MASS, public domain", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/st218-toy.webp", "alt": "Stephenson 2-18 as this toy draws it", "credit": "Stephenson 2-18 in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/st218-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/ALMA_CO_line_emission_of_Stephenson_2_DFK_1.png/960px-ALMA_CO_line_emission_of_Stephenson_2_DFK_1.png", "alt": "ALMA map of carbon monoxide gas around Stephenson 2-18: coloured contour rings around a star symbol at the centre.", "credit": "M. A. Siebert et al., CC BY 4.0", "after": 4}] },
  wohg64: { text: "WOH G64 is a giant star that belongs to the Large Magellanic Cloud, a small galaxy that travels around our own Milky Way. Its distance from us is about 163,000 light years. It does not live alone: it has a smaller, hotter partner, a star of type B. The pair is a symbiotic binary, one in which the big star loses gas to its partner.\n\nThe main star is a red supergiant, a cool and hugely swollen star, with a surface of only about 3,400 K. It could be the biggest star whose size has been measured well, at about 1,540 times the Sun’s radius. If it took the Sun’s place, its surface would reach beyond the orbit of Jupiter. Even light would need more than six hours to travel once around it. It has about 20 times the Sun’s mass and shines about 282,000 times as brightly. Older estimates, which assumed a round shell of dust, made it brighter and up to 3,000 times the Sun’s radius. The dust is really a ring seen from above, so light escapes through its hole straight towards us, and the star looked brighter than it is.\n\nThe star is losing mass fast through a strong wind, about 3 to 7 ten-thousandths of the Sun’s mass each year. This lost material has built a thick shell of dust about one light year across, as heavy as 3 to 9 Suns. In 2007 the Very Large Telescope showed that the dust is shaped like a ring or a doughnut. Astronomers call this shape a torus.\n\nBengt Westerlund, Nils Olander and B. Hedin found the star in the 1970s, and the letters WOH come from their surnames. In 2024 the VLTI, which combines the light of several large telescopes, made a close-up picture of the dust around it. It was the first picture of this kind of a star in another galaxy.\n\nEmily Levesque and her team suspected a hot partner as early as 2009, because glowing gas near the star moves about 50 km each second differently from the star itself. Since 2016 the light of WOH G64 has shown signs of its partner, which pulls gas off the giant. Around 2014 the way its brightness changes became irregular. In 2024 one team said the star had turned into a yellow hypergiant about half its old size. A 2026 study by van Loon and Ohnaka disagreed. It found titanium oxide, the mark of a cool star, in spectra from 2024 and 2025, so the star remains a red supergiant, and a close pass of the partner had only stretched its outer layers for a while.", photos: [{"src": "photos/wohg64.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/WOH_G64_VLTI.jpg/960px-WOH_G64_VLTI.jpg", "alt": "The VLTI close-up of WOH G64: a glowing oval blob, the star in its dust cocoon, inside a fainter loop of dust.", "credit": "ESO/K. Ohnaka et al., CC BY 4.0", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/wohg64-toy.webp", "alt": "WOH G64 as this toy draws it", "credit": "WOH G64 in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/wohg64-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Magellanic_Cloud.jpg/960px-Magellanic_Cloud.jpg", "alt": "Spitzer infrared image of the Large Magellanic Cloud: a wide cloud of glowing dust and countless stars.", "credit": "NASA/JPL-Caltech/M. Meixner (STScI) & the SAGE Legacy Team, public domain", "after": 0}] },
  quasi: { text: "A quasi-star is a kind of giant star that may have lived when the universe was very young. None has been found. It is an idea from computer models, first put forward in 2006. Today’s stars are powered by fusion in their centres. A quasi-star would instead get its energy from gas plunging into a central black hole, so some call it a \"black hole star\".\n\nIt could only form early on, when the universe held almost nothing but hydrogen and helium, so it may have been one of the very first stars, called Population III. Huge clumps of dark matter would pull in vast amounts of gas. In the middle of a newborn star of at least 1,000 times the Sun’s mass, the core would collapse into a black hole. In a normal star this would blow the outer layers away as a supernova. Here the outer layers are so heavy that they soak up the blast and stay put.\n\nInside, the black hole would swallow gas from a thick disc around it as fast as it could. The infalling gas gives off so much light that its pressure holds up the outer envelope, which carries most of the mass, the way fusion holds up a normal star. The envelope churns, carrying the extra heat outward by convection.\n\nThe result would be enormous. Models give a radius of about 8,200 to 40,700 times the Sun’s, the size of our Solar System or larger. The surface would be hotter than 4,000 K, and the whole object would shine as brightly as a small galaxy. Seen from outside, it would look much like a red giant, only vastly larger.\n\nA quasi-star would not last long, perhaps 7 to 10 million years. In that time its black hole could grow to between 1,000 and 10,000 times the Sun’s mass. Once the star cooled to about 4,000 K it could no longer hold together and would drift apart, with no explosion, leaving the black hole behind. Such black holes might be the seeds of the giant ones that appeared early in cosmic history.\n\nThe James Webb Space Telescope has spotted hundreds of small, very red objects in the early universe, nicknamed little red dots. Some astronomers think a few, such as one called MoM-BH*-1, could be quasi-stars, but none is confirmed. Rohan Naidu and his team argue that MoM-BH*-1, seen as it was 660 million years after the Big Bang, is a black hole star: almost all its light is red, which they explain by light scattering in thick gas.", photos: [{"src": "photos/quasi.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/An_artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_a_black-hole_star.jpg", "alt": "An artist’s impression, not a photo, of a black hole star: a vast glowing cloud of gas around a hidden black hole.", "credit": "MPIA/HdA/T. Müller/A. de Graaff, CC BY 4.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/quasi-toy.webp", "alt": "Quasi-star as this toy draws it", "credit": "Quasi-star in this toy", "after": 4}, {"src": "photos/quasi-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Little_Red_Dots_%28NIRCam_Image%29_%282025-101%29.png/960px-Little_Red_Dots_%28NIRCam_Image%29_%282025-101%29.png", "alt": "Six James Webb Space Telescope close-ups of little red dots: small red points of light on black, each labelled with its survey name.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI, Dale Kocevski (Colby College), public domain", "after": 5}] },
  wr140: { text: "WR 140 is a pair of very hot, heavy stars in the constellation Cygnus. It lies about 5,000 light years away, in the middle of the triangle made by Deneb, Gamma Cygni and Delta Cygni. One star is a Wolf–Rayet star, a massive star late in its life that is blowing off its outer layers. The other is a big, bright O-type star. It has been called the brightest Wolf–Rayet star in the northern sky.\n\nThe two stars swing around each other every 7.9 years on a long, stretched orbit. At their closest they are 1.3 AU apart, a little more than the gap between Earth and the Sun. At their farthest they are 23.9 AU apart. The O star holds about 29 times the Sun’s mass and the Wolf–Rayet star about 10. Astronomers worked out the orbit from the stars’ spectral lines, which shift back and forth as each star moves towards us and then away.\n\nBoth stars blow strong winds of gas. The Wolf–Rayet star’s wind is rich in carbon and has almost no hydrogen. Shortly after the stars pass closest, where the two winds crash together, the system makes a fresh burst of dust rich in carbon, a kind of soot, and grows much brighter in infrared light. Ultraviolet light from the stars heats the new grains to about 1,000 K, so they glow. This happens about every eight years. New bursts were seen in 1985, 1993, 2001 and 2009. Each burst adds a new shell, so the stars sit inside a set of rings, one inside the next.\n\nThe first shells were seen from the Keck Observatory around 1999 to 2001, but telescopes on the ground could only pick out one or two. In 2022 the James Webb Space Telescope saw more than 17 shells with MIRI, its mid-infrared camera, reaching about 70,000 AU from the stars. Together they record over 130 years of dust. The grains form about 50 AU from the stars and leave at about 1,800 km each second. The light of the stars keeps pushing them faster, until at about 220 AU the dust thins out and the push weakens. The glow of the shells hints at polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, tough ring-shaped carbon molecules.\n\nWR 140 is the classic example of a colliding-wind binary, a pair whose winds crash together, and of how stars spread dust into space. Most Wolf–Rayet stars wrap their dust in spirals, so these neat rings are odd. No one is yet sure why they are so regular.", photos: [{"src": "photos/wr140.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/WR140.png/960px-WR140.png", "alt": "James Webb Space Telescope infrared image of WR 140, a bright star with spikes, wrapped in many thin, nested rings of dust.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA / JWST MIRI, Ryan Lau et al., processed by Meli thev, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 2}, {"src": "photos/wr140-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Observations_of_Wolf-Rayet_140_%28MIRI_Images%2C_clean%29_%28weic2501b%29.jpg/960px-Observations_of_Wolf-Rayet_140_%28MIRI_Images%2C_clean%29_%28weic2501b%29.jpg", "alt": "Close-up of two Webb images of the WR 140 dust rings, taken 14 months apart in July 2022 and September 2023, joined along a diagonal: the rings no longer line up across the seam, because the dust has moved outwards.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI, E. Lieb, R. Lau, J. Hoffman, CC BY 4.0", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/wr140-toy.webp", "alt": "WR 140 as this toy draws it", "credit": "WR 140 in this toy", "after": 4}] },
  vfts352: { text: "VFTS 352 is a pair of stars in the Tarantula Nebula, a huge cloud where new stars form. The nebula lies inside the Large Magellanic Cloud, a small galaxy close to our own, some 160,000 light years from us. In our sky it is in the southern constellation Dorado.\n\nThe two stars sit so close together that their surfaces touch and their outer layers overlap, joined by a bridge of gas. Astronomers call this a contact binary. No heavier pair of this kind is known: together they hold about 57 times the Sun’s mass. Each star has about 29 times the Sun’s mass and about 7 times its radius. Their centres are only about 12 million km apart, and they swing around each other in about 27 hours. Each star also turns once per orbit, so they always show each other the same face.\n\nBoth are O-type stars, the hottest and heaviest kind of ordinary star. Their surfaces are about 42,500 K and 41,100 K, and each shines 150,000 to 180,000 times as brightly as the Sun. They are very young, only about 1 million years old.\n\nThe two stars are almost the same size, so neither one pulls gas away from the other. Instead they share it: about 30 per cent of their material is thought to be held in common. Their strong tides probably stir and mix the gas deep inside them.\n\nThe pair was found with the Very Large Telescope of ESO, the European Southern Observatory, during the VLT FLAMES Tarantula Survey of more than 900 stars. The team, led by Leonardo Almeida of the University of São Paulo and Hugues Sana of the University of Leuven, made it public on 13 October 2015. Massive stars like these matter to us. They make most of the oxygen in the universe deep inside them, and when they explode as supernovae they scatter it into space.\n\nNo one knows how the story will end, but there are two likely paths. The stars may merge into one star that spins very fast. Such a star might die in a long burst of gamma rays, the most energetic kind of light. Or each star may explode as a supernova and leave a black hole behind. The two black holes would then circle each other closely, and if they later merge, they would send out gravitational waves, ripples in space itself.", photos: [{"src": "photos/vfts352.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Location_of_VFTS_352_in_the_Large_Magellanic_Cloud_%28eso1540b%29.tiff/lossy-page1-960px-Location_of_VFTS_352_in_the_Large_Magellanic_Cloud_%28eso1540b%29.tiff.jpg", "alt": "A telescope image of the Tarantula Nebula, with red crosshairs marking VFTS 352 at the centre.", "credit": "ESO/M.-R. Cioni/VISTA Magellanic Cloud survey, CC BY 4.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/vfts352-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/An_artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_the_hottest_and_most_massive_touching_double_star.jpg/960px-An_artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_the_hottest_and_most_massive_touching_double_star.jpg", "alt": "An artist’s impression, not a photo, of VFTS 352: two glowing blue-white stars so close that their surfaces touch and merge in the middle.", "credit": "ESO/L. Calçada, CC BY 4.0", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/vfts352-toy.webp", "alt": "VFTS 352 as this toy draws it", "credit": "VFTS 352 in this toy", "after": 4}] },
  xxtri: { text: "XX Trianguli is a variable star, which means a star whose brightness changes. It lies in Triangulum, a small northern constellation, close to where it meets Andromeda. It is about 640 light years away and is slowly coming closer to us, at about 26 km each second. Its magnitude moves between 8.1 and 8.7, so it is too dim to see with the eye alone. It belongs to the RS Canum Venaticorum class: close pairs with a spotted, restless star that brightens and fades as its spots turn into view and out again.\n\nThe star we see is an orange giant. It has used up the hydrogen in its core, so it has cooled and swelled to about 11 times the Sun’s width. Its mass is about 1.26 times the Sun’s, and it gives out about 30 times as much light. Its surface is about 4,620 K, and its age is about eight billion years.\n\nWhat makes XX Trianguli famous is its spots. It has dark patches on its surface, called starspots, and one of them is wider than the whole Sun. Astronomers found it with Doppler imaging, a method that tracks small shifts in the star’s light as it spins. Klaus Strassmeier reported the giant spot in 1999. Most of its spots sit near the poles and at high latitudes, with now and then a few small ones near the middle. Later, a team including Strassmeier watched the star for six years with STELLA, a 1.2 m robotic telescope on Tenerife, and made 36 maps, one each turn. They saw spots split, merge, fade and form, and one active patch kept facing the unseen partner.\n\nIt spins once in about 24 days, in step with its orbit, which is quick for a star this big. Its middle and its poles turn at slightly different speeds, much as on the Sun, but the difference is small. Its magnetic activity also seems to rise and fall in a cycle of about 26 years. Only one such cycle has been watched so far, so this is not yet sure. A 2025 study of forty years of measurements found that the star has also slowly grown brighter and hotter.\n\nThe star also has a partner that nobody can see. We know it is there only because the giant’s light shifts back and forth as the pair goes around each other every 24 days. Brian Skiff, of Lowell Observatory, probably first noticed the star’s changing brightness in 1985. It got its variable star name, XX Trianguli, in 1993.", photos: [{"src": "photos/xxtri-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/XXTriLightCurve.png/960px-XXTriLightCurve.png", "alt": "A light curve of XX Trianguli from late 2015 to early 2016: its brightness rises and falls between about magnitude 7.9 and 8.5 roughly every 24 days, as the spots turn past.", "credit": "PopePompus, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/xxtri.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/12545_b.jpg/960px-12545_b.jpg", "alt": "Four Doppler maps of XX Trianguli (HD 12545) seen from different sides, with a huge dark starspot over the pole and the Sun drawn small for scale.", "credit": "K. Strassmeier / NOAO/AURA/NSF, CC BY 4.0", "after": 2}, {"src": "photos/xxtri-toy.webp", "alt": "XX Trianguli as this toy draws it", "credit": "XX Trianguli in this toy", "after": 3}] },
  epsaur: { text: "Epsilon Aurigae is a star system in Auriga, the Charioteer, close in the sky to the bright star Capella. Its distance is still argued over, but measurements by the Gaia spacecraft put it near 3,300 light years. The bright star, named Almaaz, is a yellow-white supergiant that is easy to see with the naked eye, even from towns.\n\nWhat makes it famous is its eclipse. About every 27 years, something dark passes in front of the bright star and its brightness drops from magnitude 2.92 to 3.83. Each eclipse lasts close to two years, 640 to 730 days. The dark partner gives off very little light for its size. Today most astronomers think it is a thick disc of dust about 3.8 times as wide as the distance from Earth to the Sun, wrapped around a hidden star, probably a hot B-type star or a pair of them. The disc is about 0.475 AU thick and blocks about 70 per cent of the light that passes through it, so some starlight still gets through.\n\nThe star has puzzled people for two centuries. Johann Fritsch first noticed its changes in 1821. In 1904 Hans Ludendorff showed that it was an eclipsing pair. In 1937 Gerard Kuiper, Otto Struve and Bengt Strömgren suggested the dark partner was a huge, see-through star. In 1940 Sergei Gaposchkin put its radius near 2,400 times the Sun’s, which would have made it the biggest star known. A black hole was suggested too. Both ideas were later dropped. In 1965 Su-Shu Huang argued instead for a large disc seen edge on.\n\nThe masses are still unclear. In one model the bright star is a supergiant of about 15 times the Sun’s mass; in another it holds only about 2 to 4 times the Sun’s mass. Its radius may be anywhere from about 143 to 358 times the Sun’s. It also pulses gently, over periods of about two to four months. The orbit is tilted more than 87 degrees, so we see it almost edge on. In the heavy model the two stars are about 35 AU apart, farther than Neptune is from the Sun; in the light one, only 18 AU.\n\nThe CHARA telescope array caught the dark disc in outline during the last eclipse, between 2009 and 2011. The slight brightening in mid-eclipse turned out to come from the disc’s flared shape, which scatters some starlight towards us. A weaker eclipse, when the disc passes behind the star, is expected from late 2025 to early 2028.", photos: [{"src": "photos/epsaur.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Epsilon_Aurigae.jpg/960px-Epsilon_Aurigae.jpg", "alt": "An artist’s impression, not a photo, of Epsilon Aurigae: a bright star beside the dark dusty disc that eclipses it.", "credit": "NASA/JPL-Caltech, public domain", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/epsaur-toy.webp", "alt": "Epsilon Aurigae as this toy draws it", "credit": "Epsilon Aurigae in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/epsaur-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Epsilon_Aurigae_eclipse_light_curve.png/960px-Epsilon_Aurigae_eclipse_light_curve.png", "alt": "A light curve of the 2009 to 2011 eclipse of Epsilon Aurigae: green measurements drop as the eclipse begins, stay low with a slight rise in the middle, then climb back.", "credit": "Lithopsian, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 4}] },
  mothra: { text: "Mothra is a pair of giant stars in a faraway galaxy, seen in the direction of the constellation Eridanus. It is some 10.5 billion light years from us. Few star systems this far away have ever been found. Its redshift, a measure of how much the expanding universe has stretched its light, is 2.091.\n\nA star so far away should be far too faint to see on its own. We can see Mothra because of gravitational lensing: the gravity of a heavy object in front of it bends and boosts its light, like a giant magnifying glass. Its home galaxy lies behind a huge cluster of galaxies called MACS J0416.1−2403, which has the nickname Christmas Tree Galaxy Cluster.\n\nThe extra boost for Mothra comes from a small unseen object very close to our line of sight, called a millilens. It has between 10,000 and a million times the Sun’s mass. It may be a small globular cluster, a tight ball of old stars, or a clump of dark matter, matter that we cannot see. It is one of the smallest lumps of matter ever found this far away by lensing. Single stars in the cluster cannot explain the older Hubble pictures of Mothra, so a larger lump is needed. A lump this small fits the standard model of cold, slow-moving dark matter, and it rules out warm dark matter made of particles lighter than 8.7 keV.\n\nMothra is two stars. The larger is a yellow supergiant or hypergiant with a radius of about 271 R☉ and a surface of about 5,250 K. It began life with about 15 times the Sun’s mass. Its partner is a blue supergiant about 60 R☉ in radius and about 14,000 K. The blue star is the brighter of the two, at about 125,000 times the Sun’s light, against 50,000 for the yellow one.\n\nThe Spanish astrophysicist Jose Diego gave it the nickname Mothra, a nod to how monstrous it is. His team reported it in 2023, in James Webb Space Telescope images from the PEARLS programme. Hubble had already caught it, so it has stayed in view for at least eight years. It also goes by LS1, short for lensed star 1. Its brightness may change over time. As of 2026, no one knows how the two stars move around each other.", photos: [{"src": "photos/mothra.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Galaxy_cluster_MACS0416_with_Mothra_pullout_%28heic2310b%29.jpg/960px-Galaxy_cluster_MACS0416_with_Mothra_pullout_%28heic2310b%29.jpg", "alt": "Image of the galaxy cluster MACS0416 with a close-up box on the stretched, lensed background galaxy that holds Mothra.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI, J. Diego et al., CC BY 4.0", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/mothra-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/MACS_0416_%28Hubble_and_Webb_Compared%29_%282023-146%29.png/960px-MACS_0416_%28Hubble_and_Webb_Compared%29_%282023-146%29.png", "alt": "The galaxy cluster MACS0416 seen twice side by side: by Hubble in visible light on the left, and by Webb in infrared on the right, which shows more faint red galaxies.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI, public domain", "after": 4}] },
  uhz1: { text: "UHZ1 is a young galaxy seen as it was about 470 million years after the Big Bang. Its light left 13.2 billion years ago. It lies behind Abell 2744, a giant cluster of galaxies also called Pandora's Cluster. The cluster's gravity bends and magnifies the light behind it about four times, so the James Webb Space Telescope could see UHZ1 as a small red dot.\n\nThe Chandra X-ray Observatory saw hot X-rays from the same dot: gas falling into a black hole of about 40 million suns. That is far too heavy to have grown from a dead star so soon. So UHZ1 is strong evidence that some of the first black holes were born heavy, from a giant cloud of gas that collapsed straight down. A 2026 study doubts it, and reads the dot as a young galaxy poor in metals, with no feeding hole.\n\nIn the Chandra picture the X-rays are purple: UHZ1 is a hazy neon purple oval with a light pink core." },
  momz14: { text: "MoM-z14 is the most distant galaxy confirmed so far. We see it as it was about 280 million years after the Big Bang, when the universe was about two percent of its age today. The James Webb Space Telescope found it in 2025, and a spectrum fixed its redshift at 14.44.\n\nIt is small, a few hundred light years across, yet bright for so early a time, and rich in nitrogen. No cluster lies in front to magnify it. To the eye of the telescope it is only a faint red smudge, its light stretched from ultraviolet to infrared on its long way here." },
  eincross: { text: "The Einstein Cross is one quasar seen four times. The quasar, a black hole feeding on gas, lies about 8 billion light years away in Pegasus. Exactly in front of it, twenty times nearer, sits a spiral galaxy called Huchra's Lens. The galaxy's core bends the quasar's light round itself on four paths, so four images of the quasar stand in a cross round the core, only 1.6 arcseconds apart.\n\nEinstein predicted that mass bends light. Here the lens is a galaxy, and its stars add their own small lenses: as they drift across the paths, each image brightens and fades on its own, over months. A fifth faint image should sit at the centre, but the bright core hides it." },
  v838: { text: "V838 Monocerotis is a red supergiant about 19,000 light years away in Monoceros, the Unicorn. In January 2002 it suddenly flared up to about a million times the Sun's brightness, for a few weeks one of the brightest stars in our galaxy. It did not explode. The best explanation is that two stars merged into one.\n\nAfter the flash the star swelled into a cool red supergiant, among the largest stars known. Then something stranger appeared round it. The flash's light travelled out through shells of dust the star had thrown off long before, and each shell lit up as the light passed. We see this as a light echo: rings and swirls of dust that seem to grow faster than light, though nothing moves at all. Only the light moves. Hubble photographed it again and again from 2002 to 2006, and the pictures became some of its most famous.\n\nHere the echo grows on its own clock. Its outer edge shows the first, bluest light of the flash; its inner parts show the later, redder light." },
  earendel: { text: "Earendel, also called WHL0137-LS, is a very faint, very distant object in Cetus, the Whale. If it is just one star, it is the farthest star ever seen. Its light set out when the universe was only about 900 million years old, and travelled for 12.9 billion years to reach us. Because space has kept stretching, the place where it is now lies about 28 billion light years from us. Its redshift, a measure of that stretching, is about 6.2.\n\nWe can only see it thanks to a lucky line-up. A huge group of galaxies, WHL0137-08, sits between it and us. Its gravity bends and focuses Earendel’s light like a giant lens. Early studies said this made Earendel brighter by a factor of 1,000 to 40,000. The galaxy it lives in was stretched into a long curve called the Sunrise Arc.\n\nHubble found Earendel in pictures taken in 2016 and 2019, and the news was shared with the world on 30 March 2022. It beat Icarus, a lensed star Hubble found in 2018 at a redshift of 1.49. In Old English the name means the \"morning star\", also read as \"rising light\". It also nods to Eärendil, a sky-sailing character in Tolkien’s The Silmarillion. Webb took its first picture of it on 30 July 2022.\n\nIn 2023 Webb data pointed to a heavy, blue-white star of type B, with a surface over two times the Sun’s temperature and a glow roughly a million times stronger. Webb also saw signs of a cooler, red companion. As one lone star it would be 13,000 to 16,000 K and might hold 20 to 200 times the Sun’s mass. Its light sends mixed signals: a strong Balmer break, a step in the spectrum typical of stars below 13,000 K, but a blue ultraviolet glow typical of stars above 20,000 K. Two stars, one hot and one cool, could explain both.\n\nNewer work in 2025 put the lensing boost much lower, only about 43 to 67 times. Webb’s spectra now hint that Earendel is probably a cluster of many stars, perhaps a young globular cluster poor in heavy elements. The first study said it must be smaller than 0.3 parsecs, about one light year across. Another 2025 study said that limit could be ten times looser, because small clumps of dark matter may also bend its light. A single star has not been ruled out, so its true nature is still open.", photos: [{"src": "photos/earendel.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Earendel_and_the_Sunrise_Arc_in_the_galaxy_cluster_WHL0137-08_%28sunrisearc2%29.jpg/960px-Earendel_and_the_Sunrise_Arc_in_the_galaxy_cluster_WHL0137-08_%28sunrisearc2%29.jpg", "alt": "A Webb image of the galaxy cluster WHL0137-08, with an inset of the stretched Sunrise Arc galaxy where Earendel sits.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA / Webb, D. Coe, Z. Levay, CC BY 4.0", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/earendel-2.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/23/NASA-Star-Earendel-HubbleST-20220330.png", "alt": "Hubble’s discovery image: the faint red Sunrise Arc crossed by a dashed magnification line, with Earendel marked where the two meet and a mirrored star cluster on each side.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, B. Welch, D. Coe, A. Pagan, public domain", "after": 2}] },
  powehi: { text: "Pōwehi is the giant black hole at the heart of Messier 87, a huge galaxy in Virgo. Its catalogue name is M87*. The galaxy lies about 53 million light years away and holds several trillion stars. A black hole is a place where matter is packed so tightly that nothing, not even light, can get out.\n\nPōwehi is one of the heaviest black holes known. Measurements from 2019 put its mass at about 6.5 billion times the Sun’s. The edge of no return lies about 120 AU from its centre, about three times as far as Pluto is from the Sun. A disk of hot gas swirls around it at up to about 1,000 km each second. It swallows about one Sun’s worth of gas every ten years.\n\nPōwehi also shoots out a narrow jet of hot, electrically charged gas at nearly the speed of light. The jet reaches at least 4,900 light years out from the middle of the galaxy. Heber Curtis noticed it in 1918 and called it a curious straight ray. Walter Baade later found that its light is polarised, a sign of fast electrons moving through a magnetic field. In Hubble pictures from 1999, parts of the jet seemed to move at four to six times the speed of light. This is an illusion: the gas really moves at 80 to 85 per cent of light speed, partly towards us, so it nearly keeps pace with its own light.\n\nCharles Messier listed the galaxy in 1781, taking it for a faint cloud. In 2017 the Event Horizon Telescope, a set of radio telescopes around the world working as one, watched the black hole in microwaves 1.3 mm long. The team showed the result on 10 April 2019. It was the first picture ever made of a black hole, with a dark shadow inside an uneven ring of glowing gas about 690 AU wide. The ring is brightest at the bottom, where the gas swings towards us; matter moving towards us this fast looks brighter.\n\nAfter the picture came out, the black hole got the name Pōwehi. It comes from the Kumulipo, an old Hawaiian creation chant, and it speaks of a dark, endless, adorned creation. In 2021 the team showed how the ring looks in polarised light. That view reveals the magnetic fields close to Pōwehi, and these may help explain how it drives its jet. In 2023 a new way of processing the data, called PRIMO, turned the same 2017 observations into a crisper picture.", photos: [{"src": "photos/powehi.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Black_hole_-_Messier_87_crop_max_res.jpg/960px-Black_hole_-_Messier_87_crop_max_res.jpg", "alt": "The Event Horizon Telescope picture of Pōwehi: a dark shadow inside a lopsided ring of glowing orange gas.", "credit": "Event Horizon Telescope / ESO, CC BY 4.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/powehi-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/M87_jet.jpg/960px-M87_jet.jpg", "alt": "Hubble picture of the galaxy M87: a bright yellow core at top left, with a thin blue jet of gas streaming out towards the lower right.", "credit": "NASA and The Hubble Heritage Team (STScI/AURA), public domain", "after": 2}, {"src": "photos/powehi-toy.webp", "alt": "Pōwehi as this toy draws it", "credit": "Pōwehi in this toy", "after": 3}] },
  gargantua: { text: "Gargantua is the black hole of a 2014 science fiction film. It is not real. Physicists traced how its gravity bends light, and the film shows the result: a thin, hot disc of gas round a black shadow, and the far side of the disc lifted over the top and under the bottom of the shadow.\n\nIn the film it holds about 100 million suns and spins near the fastest rate possible. The bent light makes a thin bright ring at the edge of the shadow. The film left out one real effect: the side of the disc that comes toward us should be much brighter than the side that goes away. The film team kept the disc even, so it reads clearly." },
  wr124: { text: "WR 124 belongs to a rare group called Wolf-Rayet stars and lies in Sagitta, the Arrow. A Wolf-Rayet star is a very hot, heavy star that has blown away much of its outer layer of hydrogen in a fierce wind. Its distance is uncertain. Studies give figures from about 11,000 to 27,000 light years, and the Gaia spacecraft points to roughly 21,000.\n\nThe lowest figure comes from a 2010 study. Its team compared Hubble Space Telescope images of the glowing cloud around the star, taken 11 years apart, to see how far the cloud had grown across the sky. They set this against the cloud’s true speed, found from the Doppler shift, the change in the colour of light from a moving source. At that distance the star would shine only about 150,000 times as brightly as the Sun.\n\nFew stars in our galaxy flee through space as fast; it moves at around 200 km per second. Its surface is near 40,000 K, so most of its light is ultraviolet. It may shine up to a million times as brightly as the Sun. Estimates of its size run from about 12 to 26 times the Sun’s radius, and of its mass from about 20 to 33 times the Sun’s.\n\nThe star sits inside a glowing cloud called M1-67, made of gas it has thrown off. The cloud spreads outward at more than 150,000 km per hour and now measures almost 6 light years wide, which suggests it is about 20,000 years old. It holds big clumps, some weighing as much as 30 Earths and stretching as far as 150 billion km. One of them would span the gap between the Sun and Saturn.\n\nPaul W. Merrill found WR 124 in 1938 and noted its high speed. Later, in 1982, Anthony Moffat and his team found that its light changes slightly, and in 1985 it got the variable star name QR Sagittae. The changes are tiny, less than a tenth of a magnitude.\n\nWR 124 is losing mass fast, between a hundred-thousandth and a ten-thousandth of the Sun’s mass each year. Only about 15% of it is still hydrogen; most of the rest is helium. A young, very bright star of its kind would still fuse hydrogen in its core, while an older, fainter one would already fuse helium, so its true distance also tells its stage of life. Either way, its remaining life is short, at most some hundreds of thousands of years, and it should end as a supernova of the type called Ib or Ic.", photos: [{"src": "photos/wr124.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Wolf-Rayet_124_%28NIRCam_and_MIRI_composite_image%29.tif/lossy-page1-960px-Wolf-Rayet_124_%28NIRCam_and_MIRI_composite_image%29.tif.jpg", "alt": "A James Webb Space Telescope infrared image of WR 124 shining at the centre of the clumpy cloud of gas and dust it has thrown off.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI / Webb, public domain", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/wr124-toy.webp", "alt": "WR 124 as this toy draws it", "credit": "WR 124 in this toy", "after": 4}, {"src": "photos/wr124-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/M1-67_%26_WR124.png/960px-M1-67_%26_WR124.png", "alt": "A Hubble Space Telescope image of the clumpy glowing cloud M1-67, with WR 124 at its centre.", "credit": "Judy Schmidt, from Hubble Legacy Archive data, CC0", "after": 3}] },
  siriusb: { text: "Sirius B is a white dwarf, the small, faint partner of Sirius, the brightest star we see at night. The pair lies about 8.6 light years away in Canis Major, the Greater Dog. In visible light Sirius B is about 10,000 times fainter than its partner Sirius A, so it hides in the glare of its bright companion.\n\nA white dwarf is the hot, leftover core of a star that has used up its fuel. Sirius B packs about 1.02 times the Sun’s mass into a ball close to the size of Earth, about 12,000 km across. That makes it one of the heaviest white dwarfs known, nearly twice the usual mass. Its surface is about 25,200 K. Its outer layer is almost pure hydrogen. Below it lies a core of carbon and oxygen, made by helium fusion in the star it once was. Its strong gravity sorts the layers by weight, so the lightest element floats on top. Oddly, the more mass a white dwarf has, the smaller it is, which is why Sirius B is a little smaller than Earth.\n\nThe two stars circle each other every 50 years. The gap between them changes from 8.2 to 31.5 AU, about 20 AU on average, roughly as far as Uranus is from the Sun. In X-rays the roles swap, and Sirius B outshines Sirius A.\n\nIn 1844 Friedrich Bessel, a German astronomer, saw Sirius wobble as it moved and worked out that it must have an unseen partner. On 31 January 1862 Alvan Graham Clark first saw it while testing a new telescope with a 470 mm lens. In 1915 Walter Adams studied its light and found a faint whitish star. It was only the second white dwarf ever found. Its mass comes from the orbit, through Kepler’s third law, which links the size and period of an orbit to the mass inside it. A first estimate in 1910 gave 0.94 times the Sun’s mass, close to the 1.018 found in 2017. Its gravity also stretches its light slightly towards red, and that gravitational redshift gives a separate check of its mass and size.\n\nLong ago Sirius B was the bigger star of the pair. It began as a blue B-type star of about 5 times the Sun’s mass. It burned through its hydrogen, grew into a red giant, then lost its outer layers and shrank to a white dwarf. That was some 120 million years in the past. With no fuel left to burn, it will keep cooling for about two billion more years.", photos: [{"src": "photos/siriusb.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Sirius_A_and_B_Hubble_photo.jpg", "alt": "Hubble image of the glaring, overexposed Sirius A with spikes and rings, and Sirius B as a tiny dot to its lower left.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, H. Bond and M. Barstow / Hubble, public domain", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/siriusb-toy.webp", "alt": "Sirius B as this toy draws it", "credit": "Sirius B in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/siriusb-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Artist%27s_impression_of_the_sizes_of_Sirius_B_and_the_Earth.jpg/960px-Artist%27s_impression_of_the_sizes_of_Sirius_B_and_the_Earth.jpg", "alt": "An artist’s impression comparing the sizes of Sirius B, a glowing blue-white ball, and Earth: the white dwarf is a little smaller.", "credit": "NASA/ESA, CC BY 4.0", "after": 1}] },
  neutron: { text: "The Vela pulsar is what remains of a star that exploded: its tiny, fast-spinning core. It lies in Vela, the Sails, inside the glowing wreck of that explosion, the Vela supernova remnant. It is roughly 1,000 light years from Earth.\n\nA pulsar is a neutron star, a ball of matter packed so tightly that more than the Sun’s mass fits into a space smaller than a city. As it spins, beams from its poles sweep past Earth like a lighthouse, so we see pulses. The Vela pulsar turns about 11 times every second, once every 89 thousandths of a second. In radio waves it is the brightest pulsar in the sky, and it is the brightest steady source of high-energy gamma rays. In visible light it is very faint, at magnitude 23.6. Even so, it is the third brightest pulsar at these wavelengths, and its visible light flashes twice for every radio pulse. Its gamma-ray pulses reach energies as high as 20 TeV, or teraelectronvolts; only one other pulsar, in the Crab Nebula, is known to pulse in this range.\n\nThe star blew up as a Type II supernova about 11,000 to 12,300 years ago. In 1968 a team from the University of Sydney found that the pulsar belongs to the remnant. This was the first direct proof that supernovae leave neutron stars behind. At the time its spin was the fastest known for a pulsar. The debris of the blast still rushes outward at about 1,200 km each second.\n\nIn 1970 and 1971 the Uhuru X-ray satellite showed that the pulsar and Vela X, a bright radio source around it, are separate but linked. In 1980 Weiler and Panagia showed what Vela X is: a pulsar wind nebula, a cloud of fast particles blown out and lit by the pulsar. Its signals even became music: the composer Gérard Grisey used them in Le noir de l’étoile.", photos: [{"src": "photos/neutron.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1d/Vela_Pulsar_jet.jpg", "alt": "A Chandra X-ray image of the Vela pulsar inside its glowing cloud of particles, with a jet streaming from it.", "credit": "NASA/CXC/PSU/G. Pavlov et al. / Chandra, public domain", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/neutron-toy.webp", "alt": "The Vela pulsar as this toy draws it", "credit": "The Vela pulsar in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/neutron-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Vela_supernova_-_VST_-_Eso2214a.jpg/960px-Vela_supernova_-_VST_-_Eso2214a.jpg", "alt": "A VLT Survey Telescope view of the Vela supernova remnant: tangled orange and pink threads of gas among bright blue stars.", "credit": "ESO/TIMER survey, CC BY 4.0", "after": 2}] },
  tzo: { text: "A Thorne–Żytkow object is a kind of star that scientists think could exist, but none has been found for certain. Seen from outside, it would look like a red giant or a red supergiant, a huge swollen star. Hidden at its centre would be a neutron star, the tiny, dense leftover of a big star that has exploded.\n\nKip Thorne and Anna Żytkow first put forward the idea in 1977. Such an object could form when a neutron star crashes into a giant star. This could happen in a crowded star cluster, or in a pair of stars after one of them explodes as a supernova: the blast can knock the neutron star onto a path that runs into its partner, or the partner can later swell up and swallow it.\n\nDeep inside, the buried neutron star would have a surface hotter than a billion K, more than nearly any star’s core. The heat would come either from fusion in the falling gas or from the squeeze of the neutron star’s gravity. Some astronomers think the rapid proton process, a chain of fast reactions known from X-ray bursts on neutron stars, could also run there. Gas falling onto it could fuse in unusual ways and make odd mixes of elements, such as extra rubidium, lithium or molybdenum, that could give the object away. If the object grew hot enough to blow off its hydrogen-rich surface, it could look instead like a Wolf-Rayet star rich in nitrogen.\n\nIn 2014 Emily Levesque and her team said that HV 2112, a star of the Small Magellanic Cloud, might be the first one found. The star seemed to hold extra molybdenum, rubidium, lithium and calcium, and it was very bright. In 2018 a group led by Emma Beasor argued against this: they found no clear excess beyond perhaps lithium, and the star was too faint. They named a different star, HV 11417, which seems to have extra rubidium, as a candidate instead. Neither is confirmed. A sure answer may need both the light of the star and the gravitational waves, tiny ripples in space, sent out by the neutron star inside.\n\nSuch an object would not last long, only about 100,000 to a million years. Even so, there could be 20 to 200 of them in the Milky Way today. In the end the star may lose its outer layers, leaving a neutron star ringed by a large disk of gas. That disk might even gather into a new star that circles the neutron star. Or the neutron star may swallow enough gas to turn into a black hole.", photos: [{"src": "photos/tzo.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/HV-2112.jpg/960px-HV-2112.jpg", "alt": "HV 2112 in the Small Magellanic Cloud, the star once put forward as a Thorne-Zytkow object; no such object is confirmed", "credit": "ESA/Hubble, Digitized Sky Survey 2, CC BY 4.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/tzo-toy.webp", "alt": "Thorne–Żytkow object as this toy draws it", "credit": "Thorne–Żytkow object in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/tzo-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Kip_S._Thorne%2C_1972.jpg/960px-Kip_S._Thorne%2C_1972.jpg", "alt": "Kip Thorne, one of the two scientists who first proposed these objects, photographed in 1972.", "credit": "A. T. Service, CC BY-SA 3.0", "after": 1}] },
  cygx1: { text: "Cygnus X-1, in Cygnus, the Swan, is a black hole about 6,000 to 7,200 light years away. It was the first source of X-rays that most astronomers agreed was a black hole. It has a partner, a blue supergiant star called HDE 226868, which sits close to Eta Cygni in the sky, half a degree away.\n\nThe black hole holds about 21 times the Sun’s mass, though other studies give about 14 to 18. It and its partner circle each other every 5.6 days, only about a fifth of the Earth to Sun distance apart. A strong wind of gas from the big star spirals into a flat disc around the black hole. Friction heats the disc’s inner region to millions of degrees, and it glows in X-rays. Two jets of fast particles shoot out at right angles to the disc.\n\nX-rays cannot get through our air, so the source was only found in 1964. Stuart Bowyer, Edward Byram, Talbot Chubb and Herbert Friedman flew Geiger counters on two Aerobee rockets launched from White Sands in New Mexico, and found eight new X-ray sources, this one among them. NASA’s Uhuru satellite, launched in 1970, then saw its X-rays flicker several times a second. So the source could be no wider than about 100,000 km, roughly Jupiter’s size.\n\nThe X-ray data could not say which star it was. In April and May 1971, Luc Braes and George Miley of Leiden Observatory found radio waves from Cygnus X-1 with the new Westerbork Synthesis Radio Telescope in the Netherlands. Robert Hjellming and Campbell Wade, at the US National Radio Astronomy Observatory, found them on their own. The radio position was sharp enough to tie the X-ray source to one star, HDE 226868. In 1972 Louise Webster and Paul Murdin at the Royal Greenwich Observatory, and Tom Bolton at the David Dunlap Observatory in Toronto, working apart, measured the Doppler shift in the star’s light as it swung round its orbit. The wobble showed an unseen partner far heavier than the largest possible neutron star, about three times the Sun’s mass. By late 1973 most experts thought it was a black hole.\n\nThe object also inspired a famous friendly bet. In 1975 Stephen Hawking bet Kip Thorne that it held no black hole. He called it insurance: if black holes did not exist, his work on them was wasted, but at least he would win. He gave in in 1990, once the evidence had grown strong.\n\nThe black hole may spin about 790 times a second. Its parent star probably had over 40 times the Sun’s mass and shed most of it in a strong wind. It may then have fallen in on itself without a big explosion, since a supernova would most likely have thrown the remnant out of the pair. The pair has existed for roughly 5 million years, and in about five billion years it should merge into one black hole.", photos: [{"src": "photos/cygx1.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Chandra_image_of_Cygnus_X-1.jpg/960px-Chandra_image_of_Cygnus_X-1.jpg", "alt": "A Chandra X-ray image of Cygnus X-1, a bright point with spokes of X-ray light spread out by the telescope’s grating.", "credit": "NASA/CXC, public domain", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/cygx1-toy.webp", "alt": "Cygnus X-1 as this toy draws it", "credit": "Cygnus X-1 in this toy", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/cygx1-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Westerbork_Synthese_Radio_Telescoop.JPG/960px-Westerbork_Synthese_Radio_Telescoop.JPG", "alt": "A row of white dish antennas of the Westerbork Synthesis Radio Telescope in the Netherlands, against a blue sky.", "credit": "Onderwijsgek, CC BY-SA 2.5 NL", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/cygx1-3.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Noao-cyg_x1.jpg/960px-Noao-cyg_x1.jpg", "alt": "A dense star field with a faint curved shell of gas, a bow shock blown by the black hole’s jet; the partner star HDE 226868 is one of the bright stars below it.", "credit": "T.A. Rector and H. Schweiker / NOIRLab/NSF/AURA, CC BY 4.0", "after": 5}] },
  sgra: { text: "Sagittarius A*, a supermassive black hole millions of times heavier than the Sun, sits at the centre of the Milky Way, our galaxy. From Earth it lies near the border of Sagittarius and Scorpius, about 27,000 light years away. Its name is said as Sagittarius A-star.\n\nIt has about 4.3 million times the Sun’s mass. A black hole’s gravity is so strong that not even light can get out. So we cannot see the black hole itself, only gas, dust and stars near it. The radio and infrared glow we pick up comes from gas that grows millions of degrees hot as it falls inward. Thick dust and gas between us and the centre of the galaxy block all of its visible light, dimming it by about 25 magnitudes, so astronomers study it in radio waves and infrared.\n\nIn May 2022 the Event Horizon Telescope, eight radio observatories spread across the world and linked together, released the first picture of the glowing ring around it. It was the second black hole ever imaged, after the one in the galaxy M87. The ring is about 64 million km across, about 46 times as wide as the Sun. The picture used data taken in 2017 and took five years to work out. That was hard because the radio glow of Sagittarius A* changes within minutes, while such pictures are usually built up from a whole night of steady signal.\n\nBack in 1933 Karl Jansky, one of the fathers of radio astronomy, picked up radio waves from the direction of Sagittarius. Bruce Balick and Robert Brown found the black hole’s own source on 13 and 15 February 1974, a small, bright spot inside that wider glow, with the baseline interferometer of the National Radio Astronomy Observatory. Brown added the star symbol to its name in 1982, because the source was \"exciting\", and in physics excited atoms are marked with an asterisk.\n\nFor decades, teams tracked stars racing around it. One star, S2, reached 7,650 km each second on its closest pass in 2018, about 120 AU from the black hole. Its light then showed the extra reddening by gravity that general relativity predicts. From these orbits they showed that only a black hole could hold so much mass in so small a space. This work won Reinhard Genzel and Andrea Ghez a share of the 2020 Nobel Prize in Physics. The black hole sometimes flares. In 2019 it suddenly shone 75 times more brightly than normal.", photos: [{"src": "photos/sgra.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/EHT_Sagittarius_A_black_hole.tif/lossy-page1-960px-EHT_Sagittarius_A_black_hole.tif.jpg", "alt": "Event Horizon Telescope radio image of Sagittarius A*: a glowing orange ring around a dark centre.", "credit": "EHT Collaboration / ESO, CC BY 4.0", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/sgra-toy.webp", "alt": "Sagittarius A* as this toy draws it", "credit": "Sagittarius A* in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/sgra-2.webp", "alt": "An infrared Very Large Telescope view of the crowded stars that orbit Sagittarius A* at the centre of the galaxy.", "credit": "ESO/MPE, CC BY 4.0", "after": 4}] },
  qso: { text: "Quasar 3C 273 lies in Virgo, the Maiden. A quasar is the blazing centre of a galaxy, powered by a giant black hole. This one is about 2.4 billion light years away, at the heart of a huge, egg-shaped galaxy. No quasar was identified before it, and none in our sky is brighter: it reaches magnitude 12.9. With a 6-inch telescope, amateurs can see it, which makes it the most distant thing most of them will ever spot.\n\nIts power is hard to imagine. In visible light it shines more than 4 trillion times as brightly as the Sun, and more than 16 times as brightly as its whole home galaxy. The energy comes from gas falling onto a supermassive black hole about 890 million times the Sun’s mass, weighed by reverberation mapping: astronomers time how long a change in the centre takes to light up the gas around it. Its absolute magnitude, the brightness it would show from 10 parsecs, is −26.7: at the distance of the star Pollux it would look nearly as bright as the Sun. Its brightness changes over days to decades. Hubble’s Advanced Camera for Surveys has blocked its glare with a coronagraph, a small mask, to show the host galaxy, which holds about 200 billion times the Sun’s mass.\n\nA jet of particles moving near the speed of light shoots out from it. The jet is about 200,000 light years long. In 1995 the Hubble Space Telescope showed it is made of bright knots with dimmer gaps between them. Its light is polarised, which shows it is synchrotron light, given off by charged particles moving near the speed of light in magnetic fields.\n\nThe name means it was object number 273 in the Third Cambridge Catalogue of radio sources, published in 1959. Cyril Hazard used the Moon passing in front of it, at the Parkes Radio Telescope in Australia, to fix its position, and a starlike point of light was found there. Its spectrum looked like no known star. Maarten Schmidt and Bev Oke showed in 1963 that its light is stretched by a redshift of 0.158, so it must be billions of light years away. That made it the first known quasar.\n\nPartly because it is so bright in radio waves, its position helps define the system astronomers use to map the sky. It can be seen from March to July from both halves of the Earth.", photos: [{"src": "photos/qso.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Best_image_of_bright_quasar_3C_273_%2810953173335%29.jpg/960px-Best_image_of_bright_quasar_3C_273_%2810953173335%29.jpg", "alt": "A Hubble image of the quasar 3C 273 as a brilliant point of light.", "credit": "ESA/Hubble & NASA, CC BY 2.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/qso-toy.webp", "alt": "3C 273 as this toy draws it", "credit": "3C 273 in this toy", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/qso-2.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1b/Quasar_3C_273.jpg", "alt": "A Hubble image of 3C 273 with the quasar’s glare blocked by a coronagraph, showing the faint host galaxy; the dark finger is part of the mask.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, A. Martel, H. Ford and the ACS Science Team, public domain", "after": 1}] },
  mira: { text: "Mira, or Omicron Ceti, is a red giant in Cetus, the Whale, about 300 light years away. Its name is Latin for wonderful, given by Johannes Hevelius in 1662 because it behaved like no other star he knew. Apart from exploding stars, and perhaps Algol, it was the first star found to change in brightness. A white dwarf, Mira B, circles it about 70 times as far out as the Earth is from the Sun, once in roughly 500 years.\n\nMira A holds about the Sun’s mass and is some 6 billion years old. It is on the asymptotic giant branch, a late stage in which a star swells and sheds its outer layers. Its surface is cool, about 2,900 to 3,200 K. As it pulses, its radius swings between about 332 and 402 times the Sun’s. It is not quite round: bright spots change its outline over months.\n\nOne pulse takes about 332 days. The star is smallest and hottest just before its peak, when it gives out some 9,000 times the Sun’s energy, mostly as infrared light our eyes cannot see. A small rise in heat moves much more light into the visible range, so to the eye the change is huge. Mira has peaked as bright as magnitude 2.0 and sunk as faint as 10.1, a swing of up to 1,700 times; in infrared it changes by only about two magnitudes. Some 6,000 to 7,000 known stars pulse like this, and they are called Mira variables after it.\n\nDavid Fabricius noticed it on 3 August 1596. He was following a planet and chose a nearby third magnitude star to compare it with. By 21 August that star had grown a magnitude brighter, and by October it had faded from view. He took it for a nova, a new star, but saw it again in 1609. In 1638 Johannes Holwarda found that it came back about every eleven months. Hubble picked out the companion in 1995, and the Chandra X-ray Observatory later showed gas flowing over a bridge from the giant to the white dwarf.\n\nMira also races through space at about 130 km each second. Its wind piles up a bow shock, a curved wave like the one in front of a moving boat. In 2007 a team led by Christopher Martin reported ultraviolet images from NASA’s GALEX telescope that showed a tail behind it 13 light years long, made of gas shed over the past 30,000 years. Within a few million years Mira will throw off its outer layers as a planetary nebula and leave a white dwarf behind.", photos: [{"src": "photos/mira.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e8/Mira_1997.jpg", "alt": "Hubble image of Mira, a lopsided orange disc on black, taken in 1997", "credit": "Margarita Karovska (Harvard-Smithsonian CfA) and NASA, public domain", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/mira-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Mira_the_star-by_Nasa_alt_crop.jpg/960px-Mira_the_star-by_Nasa_alt_crop.jpg", "alt": "GALEX ultraviolet view of Mira’s bow shock, a bright blue head with a long wispy tail trailing to the left", "credit": "NASA/JPL-Caltech/C. Martin (Caltech)/M. Seibert (OCIW), public domain", "after": 4}, {"src": "photos/mira-toy.webp", "alt": "Mira as this toy draws it", "credit": "Mira in this toy", "after": 2}] },
  rlep: { text: "R Leporis is a deep red star in Lepus, the Hare, close to the border with Eridanus, about 1,490 light years away. It is a carbon star, a cool giant whose outer layers are rich in carbon. It is often called Hind’s Crimson Star, after the British astronomer John Russell Hind, who observed it in 1845.\n\nThe star is huge and cold. ALMA measured its disc at 14 to 18 thousandths of an arcsecond across, which at its distance gives a radius of about 710 to 910 times the Sun’s. Put in place of the Sun, it would reach three to four times as far out as the Earth. Yet it holds only about 1.0 to 1.5 M☉, so its gas is very thin. Its surface is near 2,290 K.\n\nLike Mira, it is a pulsing giant on the asymptotic giant branch, a late stage of a star’s life. Such stars can become rich in carbon near their end, as matter made deep inside is mixed up to the surface. In the cool outer air of R Leporis, carbon filters out the blue part of its light, which leaves the star a smoky red. It pulses once in about 427 days, and its brightness swings between magnitude 5.5 and 11.7. It is reddest when it is faintest, and near its peak the red is much less marked. From its period, Guandalini and Cristallo in 2013 worked out its output: about 13,200 times the Sun’s.\n\nHind reported that it looked “like a drop of blood on a black field.” In 2017 Gioia Rau and her team joined measurements from the VLTI, linked telescopes in Chile, with models of six carbon stars, R Leporis among them. Pulsing stars like it fit models with very extended, shell-like layers of gas, which lose mass in bursts rather than in a steady flow.\n\nIn 2021 ALMA, an array of 66 radio dishes in Chile, used R Leporis to test its sharpest setting. The dishes stood up to 16 km apart and were tuned to about 950 GHz, their highest frequency. A distant galaxy core near the star in the sky served as a reference to correct for the effects of the air. The result, published in 2023 by a team led by Yoshiharu Asaki, reached 5 thousandths of an arcsecond, enough to make out a 10 metre bus on the Moon.\n\nCarbon stars like R Leporis are near the end of their lives. Their strong winds carry carbon and other elements made inside them out into space, where later stars can form from them.", photos: [{"src": "photos/rlep.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/R_Leporis_as_imaged_by_ALMA.tif/lossy-page1-500px-R_Leporis_as_imaged_by_ALMA.tif.jpg", "alt": "ALMA image of R Leporis: a glowing orange disc in a blue haze, with a bright spot near its lower edge", "credit": "ALMA (ESO/NAOJ/NRAO)/Y. Asaki et al., CC BY 4.0", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/rlep-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/John_Russell_Hind._Photograph_by_Maull_%26_Polyblank._Wellcome_V0026558.jpg/960px-John_Russell_Hind._Photograph_by_Maull_%26_Polyblank._Wellcome_V0026558.jpg", "alt": "Sepia photograph of the astronomer John Russell Hind, seated in an armchair", "credit": "Maull & Polyblank, Wellcome Collection, CC BY 4.0", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/rlep-toy.webp", "alt": "R Leporis as this toy draws it", "credit": "R Leporis in this toy", "after": 2}] },
  ring: { text: "The Ring Nebula, also Messier 57, is a glowing shell of gas in Lyra, the Lyre, about 2,570 light years away, between the stars Beta and Gamma Lyrae. At magnitude 8.8 it is too faint for the naked eye, but a small telescope shows an oval ring. It is a planetary nebula: the cast-off outer layers of a dying star, lit by the hot core left behind.\n\nThe nebula is shaped more like a rugby ball than a ring, with most of its gas packed round its middle. We look at it within about 30 degrees of its long axis, so that thick waist shows as a ring. The blue-green inside is oxygen that has lost two electrons. This light is called forbidden, because it only escapes from gas so thin that it holds no more than a few thousand atoms in each cubic centimetre. The red towards the edge comes from hydrogen and nitrogen.\n\nAt the centre is the star that made it. Within the last two thousand years it left the stage of swollen red giants, and it no longer makes energy by fusion. What is left is a core of carbon and oxygen of about 0.61 M☉, becoming a white dwarf. Its surface is about 125,000 K, and its light makes the gas round it glow. The gas moves outwards at 20 to 30 km each second, and has done so for about 1,610 years.\n\nCharles Messier found it in January 1779, while searching for comets. Antoine Darquier de Pellepoix found it on his own weeks later and likened it to a planet fading away, which may have helped give planetary nebulae their name. In 1864 William Huggins split its light into a spectrum and saw the bright lines of glowing gas, not starlight. Eugene von Gothard, in Hungary, took the first photograph in 1886, and that September found the central star on his plates.\n\nThe James Webb Space Telescope pictured it in 2023, showing a faint outer layer of hydrogen. In 2025 Raghvendra Sahai and his team found with Webb a cloud of dust round the central star, about 2,600 times the Earth to Sun distance across, made of very small grains of silicate, the stuff of rock. The star’s light may destroy some of it quickly, so it may be topped up by comets or small rocky bodies grinding together. The star also flickers, perhaps because a small companion circles close to it.", photos: [{"src": "photos/ring.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Webb_captures_detailed_beauty_of_Ring_Nebula_%28NIRCam_image%29_%28weic2320b%29.jpg/960px-Webb_captures_detailed_beauty_of_Ring_Nebula_%28NIRCam_image%29_%28weic2320b%29.jpg", "alt": "Webb near-infrared image of the Ring Nebula: a green-blue centre inside a thick ring of orange and red filaments, with faint rays beyond", "credit": "ESA/Webb, NASA, CSA, M. Barlow, N. Cox, R. Wesson, CC BY 4.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/ring-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/The_Ring_Nebula_M57_Goran_Nilsson_%26_The_Liverpool_Telescope.jpg/960px-The_Ring_Nebula_M57_Goran_Nilsson_%26_The_Liverpool_Telescope.jpg", "alt": "Deep telescope image of the Ring Nebula: a small blue and white ring inside faint red outer shells, in a field of stars", "credit": "Göran Nilsson & The Liverpool Telescope, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/ring-toy.webp", "alt": "The Ring Nebula as this toy draws it", "credit": "The Ring Nebula in this toy", "after": 2}] },
  crab: { text: "The Crab Nebula, Messier 1, in Taurus, the Bull, is the wreck of a star that blew apart. It lies about 6,500 light years away in the Perseus Arm of the Milky Way. At magnitude 8.4 it is too faint for the naked eye, but binoculars can pick it out on a good night.\n\nThe cloud is about 11 light years across and still grows at about 1,500 km each second. In visible light it is an oval web of filaments round a soft blue centre. The filaments are the torn outer layers of the old star, mostly ionised helium and hydrogen, with carbon, oxygen, nitrogen, iron, neon and sulphur. They are between 11,000 and 18,000 K.\n\nAt the centre spins the Crab Pulsar, a neutron star only 28 to 30 km wide that turns 30.2 times a second and sends a pulse to us every 33 milliseconds. As it loses spin, the energy it sheds powers the whole nebula. Fast electrons curl round its magnetic field and give off synchrotron light, the glow of charged particles bent by a field. That is the blue haze, which Iosif Shklovsky explained in 1953. Where the pulsar’s wind hits the cloud, wisps move out at about half the speed of light, so the inner nebula changes in days.\n\nChinese astronomers recorded a new star on 4 July 1054, bright enough to see by day. Japanese and Arab observers wrote it down too. At its peak it outshone Venus, and it stayed visible to the naked eye for about two years. John Bevis found the nebula in 1731. In 1758 Charles Messier, hunting for Halley’s Comet, came across it and made it the first entry in his catalogue of cloudy objects that are not comets. In the early 1840s Lord Rosse drew it at Birr Castle with a 36 inch telescope, and his sketch, with arms like a crab’s, gave the Crab its name.\n\nIn 1921 Carl Lampland saw its shape change and John Charles Duncan showed it was growing. In 1928 Edwin Hubble proposed that it came from the star of 1054, and Nicholas Mayall later settled the case. It was the first object tied to a supernova on record. In 1968 Richard Lovelace and colleagues at Arecibo timed its pulsar, which proved that pulsars are spinning neutron stars.\n\nThe nebula sits only about 1.5 degrees from the Sun’s yearly path across the sky, so the Moon, and now and then a planet, passes in front of it. On 4 January 2003 Saturn crossed it for the first time since 1295. The Chandra X-ray Observatory watched its moon Titan go across and measured Titan’s air as about 880 km thick.", photos: [{"src": "photos/crab.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Crab_Nebula.jpg/960px-Crab_Nebula.jpg", "alt": "Hubble mosaic of the Crab Nebula: a web of orange and red filaments round a glowing blue-white centre.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, J. Hester and A. Loll (Arizona State University), public domain", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/crab-2.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/78/M1rosse.jpg", "alt": "Lord Rosse’s 1844 drawing of the nebula, a bright mass with long feathery arms, shown light on dark.", "credit": "William Parsons, 3rd Earl of Rosse, public domain", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/crab-toy.webp", "alt": "The Crab Nebula as this toy draws it", "credit": "The Crab Nebula in this toy", "after": 2}] },
  magnetar: { text: "SGR 1806-20 is a magnetar, a neutron star with an extremely strong magnetic field. It lies about 42,000 light years away in Sagittarius, on the far side of the Milky Way, and it is completely hidden in visible light. It belongs to a cluster of stars named after it, inside W31, one of the largest clouds of glowing hydrogen in our galaxy. Its neighbours include Wolf–Rayet stars, two blue hypergiants and LBV 1806-20, one of the most massive stars known in the galaxy.\n\nThe star is no more than 20 km across and turns once every 7.56 seconds, so its equator moves at about 8 km each second. Its field is over 10¹⁵ gauss. The Sun’s field is 1 to 5 gauss and the Earth’s under 1. As of 2016 nothing more magnetic had ever been seen. It could wipe a credit card from halfway to the Moon.\n\nIt is a soft gamma repeater, a star that flares in gamma rays again and again at random. Its bursts are thought to come from starquakes, when the star’s solid crust cracks. Field lines then snap and join up again, a process called magnetic reconnection that also drives flares on the Sun, and they release a burst of energy.\n\nIt was found in 1979. In 2002 a team led by Alaa Ibrahim, then a doctoral student at George Washington University, studied its bursts with NASA’s Rossi X-ray Timing Explorer. A feature in the X-rays matched the energy of protons trapped in a field of 10¹⁵ gauss. This was the first direct measurement of a magnetar’s field.\n\nOn 27 December 2004 a giant flare from the star reached the Earth. In a tenth of a second it gave out more energy than the Sun does in 150,000 years. For that moment it outshone the full Moon, though only in gamma rays, which our eyes cannot see, and it was the brightest event ever seen from outside the solar system until a gamma-ray burst in 2008. It briefly swelled the Earth’s ionosphere, the charged top of our air. NASA’s Swift satellite, only about a month in space, caught it, and David Palmer of Los Alamos led the paper. With the Very Large Array, Bryan Gaensler’s team saw debris fly out at three tenths of the speed of light. Smaller pulses after the flash suggest hot spots on its turning surface.\n\nSuch a blast within about 10 light years would strip away much of our ozone layer. The nearest known magnetar is about 9,000 light years away. A 2024 paper even proposed a rocky planet on a wildly stretched orbit to explain some bursts.", photos: [{"src": "photos/magnetar.webp", "full": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/1806-20_cluster.jpg", "alt": "A Hubble near-infrared image of the crowded star cluster 1806-20, which holds the magnetar, among many bright stars.", "credit": "Lithopsian, from HST NICMOS data, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/magnetar-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/SGR_1806-20_108530main_cloudballPrint.jpg/960px-SGR_1806-20_108530main_cloudballPrint.jpg", "alt": "Artist’s impression of the 2004 giant flare: a pale blue shell of gas spreading out round the bright point of the magnetar.", "credit": "NASA, public domain", "after": 4}, {"src": "photos/magnetar-toy.webp", "alt": "SGR 1806-20 as this toy draws it", "credit": "SGR 1806-20 in this toy", "after": 2}] },
  polaris: { text: "Polaris, the North Star, is the brightest star in Ursa Minor, the Little Bear. It sits less than one degree from the north celestial pole, the point in the sky straight above the Earth’s axis, so it hardly moves while every other northern star turns round it. It lies about 447 light years away. Many think it is the brightest star in the sky, but it ranks only 47th.\n\nIt looks like one star, but it is three. The main one is a yellow supergiant about 46 times wider than the Sun and about 5.1 times as heavy, with a surface near 6,000 K. It gives out some 1,260 times the Sun’s light and turns once in about 100 days. A small partner, Polaris Ab, circles it once in about 29 years and comes as close as 6.2 times the Earth to Sun distance. A third star, Polaris B, lies about 2,400 times that distance out.\n\nThe supergiant is a Cepheid, a star that swells and shrinks in a steady beat, here about four days long. Its brightness changes from magnitude 1.86 to 2.13. Helium in its outer layers acts as a valve. When the star is dim, that gas traps heat, warms and pushes out. As it spreads it cools, lets the heat escape, and gravity pulls it back. In 1908 Henrietta Swan Leavitt found that a Cepheid’s beat tells its true brightness, so Cepheids work as yardsticks for distance. Polaris is the closest one, so its distance helps to set the scale of the universe.\n\nWilliam Herschel found Polaris B in August 1779. In 1899 W. W. Campbell saw the main star’s speed along our line of sight change, a sign of a hidden partner. Ejnar Hertzsprung confirmed its pulsing in 1911, and in 1929 J. H. Moore published a first orbit of about 29.7 years. In 2006 the Hubble Space Telescope showed all three stars. In 2024 a team led by Nancy Evans used the CHARA array, linked telescopes on Mount Wilson, to measure the supergiant’s mass and to map what seem to be large bright and dark patches on its surface.\n\nPolaris behaves oddly for a Cepheid. After 1966 its swing in brightness shrank to under 0.05 magnitude, and it now seems to grow again, something seen in no other Cepheid. Its beat lengthened by about 4.5 seconds a year, but since about 2010 it seems to shorten. One study suggests it is 2.5 times brighter than when Ptolemy saw it.\n\nAs the Earth’s axis slowly wobbles, Polaris will come closest to the pole soon after 2100, about 0.45 degrees from it, and then drift away. The pole will return to it after some 26,000 years.", photos: [{"src": "photos/polaris.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Polaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg/960px-Polaris_alpha_ursae_minoris.jpg", "alt": "Hubble Space Telescope image of Polaris: the bright supergiant Polaris A with its close partner Polaris Ab just beside it, both labelled.", "credit": "NASA/HST, public domain", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/polaris-2.webp", "full": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Polaris_surface_image.png/960px-Polaris_surface_image.png", "alt": "A map of Polaris’s surface made with the CHARA array: an orange disc with large bright and darker patches.", "credit": "Evans et al. 2024, CC BY 4.0", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/polaris-toy.webp", "alt": "Polaris as this toy draws it", "credit": "Polaris in this toy", "after": 2}] },
};

